{"id": "reasoning-ab781da5-7f07-571f-b225-a5d44cb2fa6e-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350104486, "updatedAt": 1792350104486, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "So `_solve_static_displacement_control` (the reference solver in nonlinear_static.py, line ~3750) accepts `recover_initial_load_factor: bool = False`. The performance wrapper `_solve_static_displacement_control_block` in nonlinear_performance.py doesn't accept it, so when the public driver at nonlinear_static.py:6702 calls `_solve_static_displacement_control(...)` — which is monkeypatched to `_solve_static_displacement_control_block` at line 2015 — it raises TypeError.\n\nFix: add `recover_initial_load_factor=False` to the block signature and forward it to the reference solver in the checkpoint_path branch. But wait — the checkpoint_path branch delegates to `_ORIGINAL_DISPLACEMENT_SOLVER` when rotating_mpc_map is not None. Since recover_initial_load_factor is only True when rotating_mpc_map is not None, the checkpoint path will be taken, and forwarding to the original solver is what's needed. But should I also forward it in the non-checkpoint path? In the non-checkpoint path, recover_initial_load_factor would be False (since it requires rotating_mpc_map is not None → checkpoint_path). Actually checkpoint_path includes `rotating_mpc_map is not None`, so whenever recover_initial_load_factor could be True, checkpoint_path is True. So forwarding in the checkpoint branch suffices; but to be safe, if recover_initial_load_factor is somehow True without rotating map, we could include it in checkpoint_path condition too. Let me add `or bool(recover_initial_load_factor)` to checkpoint_path and forward the parameter.\n\nLet me check where the reference solver uses recover_initial_load_factor (line 3910).", "summary": []}
