{"id": "effect-f91d11fe-07eb-5982-80f6-c3f9ad3492ae", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350123941, "updatedAt": 1792350123970, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "offset": 1196, "limit": 420}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "content": "    theta_bar: np.ndarray,\r\n    eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    \"\"\"Return ``A(theta_bar, r_0) = d((R(theta_bar) - I) r_0) / d(theta_bar)``.\r\n\r\n    Exact closed-form derivative:\r\n        A(theta_bar, r_0) = -R(theta_bar) @ skew(r_0) @ J_r(theta_bar)\r\n    which reduces at ``theta_bar = 0`` to ``-skew(r_0)`` (i.e. ``theta x r_0``).\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    w = np.asarray(theta_bar, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    r0 = np.asarray(eccentricity, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    R = rotation_matrix_from_vector(w)\r\n    Jr = _rotation_right_jacobian(w)\r\n    return -(R @ _skew(r0) @ Jr)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian(\r\n    theta_bar: np.ndarray,\r\n    eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n    slave_force: np.ndarray,\r\n) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    \"\"\"Return the symmetric 3x3 Hessian ``d(A(theta_bar, r_0)^T f_b) / d(theta_bar)``.\"\"\"\r\n    f_b = np.asarray(slave_force, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n        return np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    w = np.asarray(theta_bar, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    r0 = np.asarray(eccentricity, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(r0)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n        return np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    H = np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    h = 1.0e-6\r\n    for j in range(3):\r\n        wp = w.copy()\r\n        wm = w.copy()\r\n        wp[j] += h\r\n        wm[j] -= h\r\n        gp = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(wp, r0).T @ f_b\r\n        gm = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(wm, r0).T @ f_b\r\n        H[:, j] = (gp - gm) / (2.0 * h)\r\n    return 0.5 * (H + H.T)\r\n\r\n\r\nclass NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry:\r\n    \"\"\"One finite-rotation eccentric beam-shell coupling descriptor.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def __init__(\r\n        self,\r\n        *,\r\n        element_id: int,\r\n        slave_u_dofs: Tuple[int, int, int],\r\n        slave_theta_dofs: Tuple[int, int, int],\r\n        masters: Tuple[Tuple[Tuple[int, ...], Tuple[int, ...], float], ...],\r\n        eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n        theta_proj: Any,\r\n        label_prefix: str,\r\n    ) -> None:\r\n        self.element_id = int(element_id)\r\n        self.slave_u_dofs = np.asarray(slave_u_dofs, dtype=np.intp)\r\n        self.slave_theta_dofs = np.asarray(slave_theta_dofs, dtype=np.intp)\r\n        self.masters = tuple(\r\n            (\r\n                np.asarray(m_u, dtype=np.intp),\r\n                np.asarray(m_theta, dtype=np.intp),\r\n                float(w),\r\n            )\r\n            for m_u, m_theta, w in masters\r\n        )\r\n        self.eccentricity = np.asarray(eccentricity, dtype=float).reshape(3).copy()\r\n        self.r_skew = _skew(self.eccentricity)\r\n        self.theta_proj = theta_proj.tocsr()\r\n        self.label_prefix = str(label_prefix)\r\n\r\n    def master_rotation(self, u: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        theta_bar = np.zeros(3, dtype=float)\r\n        for _m_u, m_theta, w in self.masters:\r\n            theta_bar += w * u[m_theta]\r\n        return theta_bar\r\n\r\n    def master_translation(self, u: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        u_bar = np.zeros(3, dtype=float)\r\n        for m_u, _m_theta, w in self.masters:\r\n            u_bar += w * u[m_u]\r\n        return u_bar\r\n\r\n\r\nclass NonlinearRotatingMPCMap:\r\n    \"\"\"State-dependent master-slave kinematic map for rotating eccentricities.\r\n\r\n    Given the reference linear constraint transformation ``T_linear`` (which\r\n    encodes ``u_b,lin = sum_i w_i u_{s,i} + theta_bar x r_0`` and\r\n    ``theta_b = theta_bar := sum_i w_i theta_{s,i}``), the nonlinear kinematic\r\n    map replaces the linearized lever-arm offset ``theta_bar x r_0`` with the\r\n    exact finite-rotation offset ``(R(theta_bar) - I) r_0``:\r\n\r\n        u_b(q)     = sum_i w_i u_{s,i}(q) + (R(theta_bar(q)) - I) r_0\r\n        theta_b(q) = theta_bar(q)\r\n\r\n    using zero Lagrange multipliers.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def __init__(\r\n        self,\r\n        T_linear: Any,\r\n        entries: Tuple[NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry, ...],\r\n    ) -> None:\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        self.T_linear = sparse.csr_matrix(T_linear, dtype=float)\r\n        self.entries = tuple(entries)\r\n        self.total_dofs = int(self.T_linear.shape[0])\r\n        self.n_red = int(self.T_linear.shape[1])\r\n\r\n    @property\r\n    def active(self) -> bool:\r\n        return bool(self.entries)\r\n\r\n    def apply_kinematics(self, u_linear: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Map a linear MPC displacement state ``u_lin`` to the nonlinear manifold.\"\"\"\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_linear, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return u\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            delta_R = _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_bar)\r\n            u[entry.slave_u_dofs] = u[entry.slave_u_dofs] + delta_R @ entry.eccentricity\r\n        return u\r\n\r\n    def to_linear_displacement(self, u_nonlinear: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Exact inverse diffeomorphism mapping ``u`` back to linear MPC coordinates.\"\"\"\r\n        u_lin = np.asarray(u_nonlinear, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return u_lin\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u_lin)\r\n            delta_R = _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_bar)\r\n            u_lin[entry.slave_u_dofs] = (\r\n                u_lin[entry.slave_u_dofs] - delta_R @ entry.eccentricity\r\n            )\r\n        return u_lin\r\n\r\n    def jacobian(self, u_current: np.ndarray) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return the state-dependent kinematic Jacobian ``J(u) = du / dq``.\"\"\"\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return self.T_linear\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        row_blocks = []\r\n        col_blocks = []\r\n        data_blocks = []\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            delta_A = A + entry.r_skew  # A(theta_bar, r_0) - (-skew(r_0))\r\n            corr = sparse.csr_matrix(delta_A) @ entry.theta_proj\r\n            coo = corr.tocoo()\r\n            if coo.nnz:\r\n                row_blocks.append(entry.slave_u_dofs[coo.row])\r\n                col_blocks.append(coo.col)\r\n                data_blocks.append(coo.data)\r\n        if not data_blocks:\r\n            return self.T_linear\r\n        delta_T = sparse.coo_matrix(\r\n            (\r\n                np.concatenate(data_blocks),\r\n                (np.concatenate(row_blocks), np.concatenate(col_blocks)),\r\n            ),\r\n            shape=(self.total_dofs, self.n_red),\r\n            dtype=float,\r\n        ).tocsr()\r\n        return (self.T_linear + delta_T).tocsr()\r\n\r\n    def reduce_force(self, u_current: np.ndarray, full_force: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``J(u)^T full_force`` without materializing ``J(u)``.\"\"\"\r\n        f = np.asarray(full_force, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return np.asarray(self.T_linear.T @ f, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        f_mod = f.copy()\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            f_b = f[entry.slave_u_dofs]\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            delta_A = A + entry.r_skew\r\n            f_mod[entry.slave_theta_dofs] += delta_A.T @ f_b\r\n        return np.asarray(self.T_linear.T @ f_mod, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n\r\n    def geometric_tangent(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        full_imbalance: np.ndarray,\r\n    ) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``K_mpc,geom(u, F_int - F_ext) = sum_b Theta_b^T H_b Theta_b``.\"\"\"\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        if not self.entries or self.n_red == 0:\r\n            return sparse.csr_matrix((self.n_red, self.n_red), dtype=float)\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        imbalance = np.asarray(full_imbalance, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        terms = []\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            if entry.theta_proj.nnz == 0:\r\n                continue\r\n            f_b = imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs]\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            H_b = rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian(\r\n                theta_bar, entry.eccentricity, f_b\r\n            )\r\n            terms.append(entry.theta_proj.T @ sparse.csr_matrix(H_b) @ entry.theta_proj)\r\n        if not terms:\r\n            return sparse.csr_matrix((self.n_red, self.n_red), dtype=float)\r\n        total = terms[0]\r\n        for term in terms[1:]:\r\n            total = total + term\r\n        return total.tocsr()\r\n\r\n    def reduce_tangent(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        K_T: Any,\r\n        full_imbalance: Optional[np.ndarray] = None,\r\n        K_ext: Optional[Any] = None,\r\n    ) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``J(u)^T (K_T - K_ext) J(u) + K_mpc,geom(u, full_imbalance)``.\"\"\"\r\n        J = self.jacobian(u_current)\r\n        K_red = (J.T @ K_T @ J).tocsr()\r\n        if K_ext is not None and getattr(K_ext, \"nnz\", 0):\r\n            K_red = (K_red - (J.T @ K_ext @ J)).tocsr()\r\n        if full_imbalance is not None:\r\n            K_geom = self.geometric_tangent(u_current, full_imbalance)\r\n            if K_geom.nnz:\r\n                K_red = (K_red + K_geom).tocsr()\r\n        return K_red\r\n\r\n    def transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        full_imbalance: np.ndarray,\r\n    ) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Transfer slave forces/moments to master shell DOFs for support reactions.\"\"\"\r\n        imbalance = np.asarray(full_imbalance, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return imbalance\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            f_b = imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs].copy()\r\n            m_b = imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs].copy()\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            moment_covector = m_b + A.T @ f_b\r\n            for m_u, m_theta, w in entry.masters:\r\n                imbalance[m_u] += w * f_b\r\n                imbalance[m_theta] += w * moment_covector\r\n            imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs] = 0.0\r\n            imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs] = 0.0\r\n        return imbalance\r\n\r\n\r\ndef has_nonlinear_rotating_mpc(model: \"FEModel\") -> bool:\r\n    \"\"\"Return True if ``model`` contains active rotating-arm beam-shell MPCs.\"\"\"\r\n    mesh = getattr(model, \"mesh\", None)\r\n    elements = getattr(mesh, \"elements\", None)\r\n    if not elements:\r\n        return False\r\n    for element in elements.values():\r\n        getter = getattr(element, \"get_nonlinear_mpc_descriptor\", None)\r\n        if callable(getter) and getter(mesh) is not None:\r\n            return True\r\n    return False\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _reject_downstream_rotating_slave_translation_dependencies(\r\n    model: \"FEModel\",\r\n    entries: Tuple[NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry, ...],\r\n) -> None:\r\n    \"\"\"Fail closed when an admitted affine equation consumes a rotating slave translation.\r\n\r\n    The rotating map corrects only each descriptor's own slave translations.\r\n    Any other admitted affine equation that uses one of those translations\r\n    as a nonzero master would silently retain the linearized lever-arm value\r\n    in the returned physical displacement, so such compositions are refused\r\n    before constitutive activation instead of being solved on the wrong\r\n    manifold.  The check uses the actual normalized audit equations,\r\n    including their coefficient filtering.  Ordinary linear chains without\r\n    a rotating descriptor and upstream shell row ties remain supported;\r\n    rotating slave rotations stay linear under the map and are not\r\n    restricted.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    rotating_slave_translations: set = set()\r\n    for entry in entries:\r\n        rotating_slave_translations.update(int(d) for d in entry.slave_u_dofs)\r\n    if not rotating_slave_translations:\r\n        return\r\n    from .constraint_audit import require_valid_constraints\r\n\r\n    report = require_valid_constraints(model)\r\n    for equation in report.equations:\r\n        if str(equation.kind) == \"support\":\r\n            continue\r\n        dependent = int(equation.dependent_dof)\r\n        for dof, coefficient in equation.coefficients:\r\n            master = int(dof)\r\n            if master == dependent or float(coefficient) == 0.0:\r\n                continue\r\n            if master in rotating_slave_translations:\r\n                raise ValueError(\r\n                    f\"Rotating-MPC route: affine equation '{equation.origin}' \"\r\n                    f\"(dependent DOF {dependent}) uses rotating slave \"\r\n                    f\"translation DOF {master} as a master. Downstream \"\r\n                    f\"dependencies on rotating slave translations are not \"\r\n                    f\"supported; remove the downstream equation or the \"\r\n                    f\"rotating coupling.\"\r\n                )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map(\r\n    model: \"FEModel\",\r\n    T_linear: Any,\r\n) -> Optional[NonlinearRotatingMPCMap]:\r\n    \"\"\"Build a :class:`NonlinearRotatingMPCMap` if rotating-arm MPCs are present.\"\"\"\r\n    from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n    mesh = getattr(model, \"mesh\", None)\r\n    elements = getattr(mesh, \"elements\", None)\r\n    if not elements:\r\n        return None\r\n    T_csr = sparse.csr_matrix(T_linear, dtype=float)\r\n    entries = []\r\n    for _elem_id, element in sorted(elements.items(), key=lambda item: int(item[0])):\r\n        getter = getattr(element, \"get_nonlinear_mpc_descriptor\", None)\r\n        if not callable(getter):\r\n            continue\r\n        desc = getter(mesh)\r\n        if desc is None:\r\n            continue\r\n        slave_theta = tuple(int(d) for d in desc[\"slave_theta_dofs\"])\r\n        theta_proj = T_csr[list(slave_theta), :]\r\n        entries.append(\r\n            NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry(\r\n                element_id=int(desc[\"element_id\"]),\r\n                slave_u_dofs=tuple(int(d) for d in desc[\"slave_u_dofs\"]),\r\n                slave_theta_dofs=slave_theta,\r\n                masters=tuple(desc[\"masters\"]),\r\n                eccentricity=np.asarray(desc[\"eccentricity\"], dtype=float),\r\n                theta_proj=theta_proj,\r\n                label_prefix=str(desc[\"label_prefix\"]),\r\n            )\r\n        )\r\n    if not entries:\r\n        return None\r\n    _reject_downstream_rotating_slave_translation_dependencies(model, tuple(entries))\r\n    return NonlinearRotatingMPCMap(T_csr, tuple(entries))\r\n\r\n", "numLines": 357, "startLine": 1196, "requestedOffset": 1196, "requestedLimit": 420, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "357 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-e9203f8e-fca4-5687-a122-656e717f5404-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350217184, "updatedAt": 1792350217184, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now I understand the current map. Key observations:\n\nCurrent map (active rotating-offset route):\n- `apply_kinematics`: `u_b = u_b,lin + (R(theta_bar)-I) r_0` where theta_bar = sum w_i theta_i (linear average). Slave orientation theta_b = theta_bar (unchanged from linear, i.e., T_linear already encodes theta_b = theta_bar).\n- `to_linear_displacement`: subtracts the same correction.\n- `jacobian`: delta_A = A(theta_bar) + skew(r_0) applied via theta_proj (frozen linear projection sum w_i T_theta_i).\n- `reduce_force`: f_mod[slave_theta] += delta_A.T @ f_b, then T_linear.T @ f_mod.\n- `geometric_tangent`: H_b = d(A^T f_b)/d theta_bar at theta_bar, contracted with theta_proj — force-only lever Hessian.\n- `transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters`: w_i f_b to master translations, w_i (m_b + A^T f_b) to master rotations.\n\nAdopted objective: replace the linear average theta_bar with intrinsic SO(3) weighted mean theta_m on the ACTIVE rotating-offset route (interp with multiple masters; direct single-master stays as-is since mean = theta_1 exactly). Connected changes per supervisor_objectivity_review.md:\n\n1. **Forward map**: \n   - translation correction: `(R_mean - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0` (subtract the ORIGINAL linear theta_lin lever, not the nonlinear mean lever).\n   - slave orientation: add `theta_m - theta_lin` to slave rotation dofs.\n   - Direct/identical limit: theta_m = theta_lin when all rotations identical or single master → corrections vanish. Good.\n\n2. **Inverse/postcheck** (`to_linear_displacement`): subtract the same master-dependent corrections from supplied physical slave translation AND rotation.\n\n3. **Jacobian**: slave translation depends on A(theta_m) @ G_i where G_i = dtheta_m/dtheta_i; slave orientation depends on G_i. The frozen theta_proj = sum w_i T_theta_i is insufficient — need individual master projections. Hmm — theta_proj is T_csr[slave_theta, :] which maps q → theta_bar = sum w_i theta_i(q). Actually T_linear rows for slave theta dofs encode theta_b = sum w_i theta_i. So d theta_lin/dq = theta_proj. For the mean, dtheta_m/dq = sum_i G_i dtheta_i/dq where dtheta_i/dq are the individual master rotation rows of T_linear. So I need per-master rotation projections: T_csr[m_theta, :] for each master.\n\n   Wait — but masters' theta dofs may themselves be slave dofs of other entries? The masters are shell node rotations, presumably free/master dofs in T_linear. The rows of T_linear for master theta dofs give dtheta_i/dq. But careful: master theta dofs could be reduced dofs themselves (e.g., if a master is itself constrained). T_linear maps q (reduced) to full u. So row m_theta of T_linear gives du[m_theta]/dq = dtheta_i/dq. Good.\n\n   So per entry, I need `master_theta_projs`: tuple of csr rows for each master's theta dofs (3 rows each). Then:\n   - delta translation block: sum_i A(theta_m) G_i dtheta_i/dq + (linear translation part unchanged) ... wait let me derive.\n\n   Forward map in linear coordinates: u_lin = T_linear q. Physical u = u_lin + corrections(q):\n   - u[slave_u] = u_lin[slave_u] + (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0\n   - u[slave_theta] = theta_lin + (theta_m - theta_lin) = theta_m\n\n   where theta_lin = sum w_i theta_i, theta_m = mean.\n\n   du[slave_u]/dq = T_linear[slave_u,:] + A(theta_m) @ sum_i G_i dtheta_i/dq - skew(r_0) @ dtheta_lin/dq\n   = T_linear[slave_u,:] + A(theta_m) @ dtheta_m/dq + skew(r_0)... \n\n   wait: -skew(theta_lin) r_0 derivative wrt theta_lin is -skew(r_0)... d(skew(theta) r_0)/d theta = -skew(r_0)? skew(theta) r_0 = theta × r_0 = -r_0 × theta = -skew(r_0) theta. So d/dtheta (skew(theta) r_0) = -skew(r_0). So derivative of -skew(theta_lin) r_0 wrt theta_lin is +skew(r_0).\n\n   Hmm wait: correction = (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0. d/dq = A(theta_m) dtheta_m/dq - (-skew(r_0)) dtheta_lin/dq = A(theta_m) dtheta_m/dq + skew(r_0) dtheta_lin/dq.\n\n   Note the old delta_A = A + r_skew where r_skew = skew(r_0): old correction was (R(theta_bar)-I) r_0 with derivative A dtheta_bar/dq, and the linear T_linear already contains -skew(r_0)... hmm, actually old delta_A = A + skew(r_0) because T_linear contains the linear lever theta_bar × r_0 = -skew(r_0) theta_bar, and the correction relative to linear is (R-I) r_0 - theta_bar × r_0 = (R-I) r_0 + skew(r_0) theta_bar. Derivative: A dtheta_bar + skew(r_0) dtheta_bar = (A + skew(r_0)) dtheta_bar. Yes, delta_A = A + r_skew.\n\n   New: correction relative to linear = (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0 (translation) + (theta_m - theta_lin) (rotation).\n   - Translation derivative: A(theta_m) dtheta_m/dq + skew(r_0) dtheta_lin/dq.\n   - Rotation derivative: dtheta_m/dq - dtheta_lin/dq = (sum_i G_i dtheta_i/dq) - theta_proj.\n\n   So the Jacobian correction:\n   - slave_u rows: A(theta_m) @ M_proj + skew(r_0) @ theta_proj, where M_proj = sum_i G_i @ T_rows(m_theta_i).\n   - slave_theta rows: M_proj - theta_proj.\n\n   For direct (single master, w=1): G_1 = I, M_proj = theta_proj → slave_theta correction zero, translation = A theta_proj + skew(r_0) theta_proj = delta_A theta_proj. Matches old behavior exactly. \n\n   For identical rotations: mean = the common rotation, G_i = w_i I (since theta_m = sum w_i theta_i when all equal... actually for identical rotations the Karcher mean is that rotation and G_i = w_i I). Then M_proj = sum w_i theta_proj_i = theta_proj. Same as direct. Good — legacy behavior retained.\n\n4. **reduce_force**: J^T f. Currently: f_mod[slave_theta] += delta_A.T @ f_b then T_linear.T @ f_mod. With the new Jacobian, J^T f needs:\n   - slave_u rows contribute: T_linear[slave_u,:]^T f_b (already in T_linear) + M_proj^T A^T f_b + theta_proj^T skew(r_0)^T f_b to slave theta... \n\n   Hmm, this is getting complex because the correction involves projections of master theta rows. Let me think in terms of the existing structure: reduce_force computes T_linear.T @ f_mod where f_mod modifies slave theta entries. The old approach: J = T_linear + delta_T where delta_T has nonzero rows only at slave_u dofs (delta_A theta_proj at slave_u rows). Then J^T f = T_linear^T f + theta_proj^T delta_A^T f_b (restricted to slave_u part f_b). They implemented it as f_mod[slave_theta] += delta_A^T f_b because T_linear^T f_mod picks up f_mod[slave_theta] through theta_proj rows: T_linear[:, slave_theta-cols]... wait.\n\n   Actually T_linear^T @ f: the slave theta rows of T_linear are theta_proj (mapping q→theta_b). So (T_linear^T f)[q] includes theta_proj^T f[slave_theta]. By adding delta_A^T f_b into f_mod[slave_theta], they get theta_proj^T delta_A^T f_b = (delta_A theta_proj)^T f_b. Correct.\n\n   New J = T_linear + delta_T_new where:\n   - delta_T_new[slave_u rows] = A M_proj + skew(r_0) theta_proj\n   - delta_T_new[slave_theta rows] = M_proj - theta_proj\n\n   J^T f = T_linear^T f + delta_T_new^T f:\n   - from slave_u: (A M_proj + skew(r_0) theta_proj)^T f_b = M_proj^T A^T f_b + theta_proj^T skew(r_0)^T f_b\n   - from slave_theta: (M_proj - theta_proj)^T m_b\n\n   Now, M_proj = sum_i G_i P_i where P_i = T_linear rows of master i's theta dofs. M_proj^T x = sum_i P_i^T (G_i^T x). And theta_proj^T y: theta_proj = sum_i w_i P_i (since theta_b = sum w_i theta_i, and T_linear row for slave theta = sum w_i * rows of master thetas). So theta_proj^T y = sum_i P_i^T (w_i y).\n\n   So J^T f = T_linear^T f + sum_i P_i^T [ G_i^T (A^T f_b) + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b + G_i^T m_b - w_i m_b ]\n   = T_linear^T f + sum_i P_i^T [ G_i^T (m_b + A^T f_b) - w_i (m_b - skew(r_0)^T f_b) ]\n\n   Hmm wait: skew(r_0)^T = -skew(r_0). Let me define v = m_b + A(theta_m)^T f_b (the slave moment covector in mean chart, including the translation lever). And the linear part: -w_i m_b + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b = -w_i (m_b - skew(r_0)^T f_b) = -w_i (m_b + skew(r_0)... hmm.\n\n   Actually, note T_linear^T f already includes the linear constraint force transfer: slave theta rows theta_proj = sum w_i P_i, so T_linear^T f includes theta_proj^T m_b = sum_i P_i^T w_i m_b, and slave_u rows include w_i f_b transfer to masters, and the linear lever: T_linear's slave_u rows contain... hmm wait, does T_linear's slave_u row include the linear lever term -skew(r_0) theta_bar? Yes: u_b,lin = sum w_i u_i + theta_bar × r_0 = sum w_i u_i - skew(r_0) theta_bar. So T_linear[slave_u] = sum w_i e_{m_u,i} - skew(r_0) theta_proj... i.e., slave_u row = w_i on master u dofs, plus -skew(r_0) @ theta_proj block.\n\n   OK so rather than decomposing, the cleanest implementation: compute delta_T_new explicitly as a sparse matrix (like jacobian() does) and compute J^T f = T_linear^T f + delta_T_new^T f. The existing reduce_force avoids materializing J for efficiency, but building the sparse delta is what jacobian() already does. For reduce_force, I can build the same delta blocks and do the transpose product directly on f:\n\n   J^T f = T_linear^T f + delta_T^T f where delta_T^T f = sum over blocks:\n   - slave_u block: (A M_proj + skew(r_0) theta_proj)^T f_b\n   - slave_theta block: (M_proj - theta_proj)^T m_b\n\n   Implementation: for each entry, compute dense 3×n_red? No — M_proj is 3×n_red sparse (sum of G_i P_i). P_i are sparse rows (3×n_red). G_i dense 3×3. So M_proj = sum_i G_i @ P_i — sparse, computed as dense-ish? Each P_i has few nonzeros (master theta dofs are typically free dofs → P_i is 3 rows with identity on 3 columns, unless master is itself reduced). Compute corr blocks as sparse: for slave_u rows: (A @ M_proj + skew(r_0) @ theta_proj) — 3×n_red sparse. For slave_theta rows: M_proj - theta_proj.\n\n   Then delta_T^T f: for each entry:\n   - contribution: C_u^T f_b + C_theta^T m_b where C_u, C_theta are 3×n_red.\n   \n   This can be done with sparse ops similar to jacobian(). Since jacobian() already builds delta_T, reduce_force can reuse the same block construction. Efficiency: fine.\n\n   Alternatively keep the f_mod trick: J^T f = T_linear^T f_mod + extra terms that don't fit the f_mod pattern (because M_proj^T A^T f_b involves P_i^T G_i^T A^T f_b — master theta rows of T_linear times a modified slave-theta-like vector, but with per-master different vectors G_i^T(A^T f_b) + G_i^T m_b - w_i(...)). Since each master has its own vector, we'd need to add to each master's theta rows... but master theta rows of T_linear map q→theta_i, and adding g_i to f_mod[m_theta_i] gives P_i^T g_i. That works! f_mod[m_theta_i] += G_i^T (A^T f_b + m_b) - w_i (m_b - skew(r_0)^T f_b)... \n\n   wait let me redo: J^T f = T_linear^T f + sum_i P_i^T [G_i^T A^T f_b + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b + G_i^T m_b - w_i m_b]\n   = T_linear^T f + sum_i P_i^T [ G_i^T (A^T f_b + m_b) - w_i (m_b - skew(r_0)^T f_b) ]\n\n   Hmm, but wait: T_linear^T f already includes sum_i P_i^T (w_i m_b) from slave theta rows and the linear lever from slave_u rows: T_linear[slave_u] includes -skew(r_0) theta_proj, so T_linear^T f includes -theta_proj^T skew(r_0)^T f_b = -sum_i P_i^T w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b.\n\n   So total J^T f = T_linear^T f + sum_i P_i^T [ G_i^T (A^T f_b + m_b) - w_i m_b + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b ] - sum_i P_i^T w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b ... \n\n   no wait. I already included the full delta_T^T f above. Let me not double count. J^T f = T_linear^T f + delta_T^T f, with delta_T^T f = sum_i P_i^T [G_i^T A^T f_b + G_i^T m_b] + theta_proj^T skew(r_0)^T f_b - theta_proj^T m_b.\n\n   Note theta_proj^T x = sum_i P_i^T w_i x. So:\n   delta_T^T f = sum_i P_i^T [ G_i^T (A^T f_b + m_b) + w_i (skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b) ].\n\n   Implementation via f_mod: f_mod[slave_theta] currently gets delta_A^T f_b in old code because slave theta rows of T_linear are theta_proj. But now the correction vectors differ per master. However, we can still use f_mod on the MASTER theta dofs: f_mod[m_theta_i] += G_i^T (A^T f_b + m_b) + w_i (skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b)? No wait — that's wrong: T_linear^T f_mod picks up P_i^T f_mod[m_theta_i] only if master theta dofs are themselves rows in T_linear with P_i. Yes! T_linear maps q → full u; row m_theta_i of T_linear is exactly P_i (dtheta_i/dq). So adding a vector g to f_mod at master theta dofs contributes P_i^T g to T_linear^T f_mod. \n\n   But careful: master theta dofs might coincide with slave theta dofs of another entry, or master u dofs... In the EP fixture, masters are shell nodes, slaves are beam nodes; distinct. But in general, could a master theta dof of entry A be a slave theta dof of entry B? Possibly in chained couplings. The old code had the same hazard with f_mod[slave_theta]. Accumulation with += handles ordering fine as long as we process consistently. But there's a subtlety: if a master theta dof is also a slave dof whose T_linear row is not P_i but the constraint row... Actually T_linear rows: for a slave dof, the row is the constraint expression; for a free dof, the row is identity. A master that is itself a slave of another MPC would have row = its own constraint. Hmm, in that case dtheta_i/dq = T_linear[m_theta_i,:] = P_i still holds — P_i is whatever the row is. Good, the f_mod approach is consistent: f_mod[m_theta] += g contributes row^T g = P_i^T g. \n\n   But wait — there's an ordering hazard: if master theta dofs of entry 1 are slave theta dofs of entry 2, then when processing entry 2 we zero... no, reduce_force doesn't zero anything; it only adds to f_mod. The old code adds to slave_theta. New code adds to master thetas. If entry 2's slave theta = entry 1's master theta, both additions accumulate — that's correct since J^T f is linear in f and each entry's contribution is additive. Fine.\n\n   Hmm, but actually there's a subtle issue with the old code's approach vs mine: old code put delta_A^T f_b at slave_theta, relying on theta_proj rows. Mine puts per-master vectors at master thetas. Both give the same result for the old map? Old: delta_T^T f = theta_proj^T delta_A^T f_b = sum_i P_i^T w_i delta_A^T f_b. Mine for old map would be sum_i P_i^T G_i^T(...) with G_i = w_i I → same. OK.\n\n   Simpler and safer: implement reduce_force by explicitly building the delta blocks (same as jacobian) and computing delta_T^T f directly. But that materializes 3×n_red sparse blocks per entry — same cost as jacobian(). reduce_force is called every Newton iteration; jacobian too. The old reduce_force avoided building J. Performance matters (budget!). The f_mod approach is cheap: per entry, per master: 3×3 matvecs. Let me use f_mod on master theta dofs.\n\n   Wait, but there's a problem: f_mod[m_theta] += ... — but then T_linear^T f_mod also picks up... no issue.\n\n   However — one more consideration: the old code also relied on T_linear^T f including the slave_u→master transfer w_i f_b (rows slave_u of T_linear have w_i at master u dofs). That's unchanged.\n\n   So new reduce_force per entry:\n   ```\n   f_b = f[slave_u]; m_b = f[slave_theta]\n   theta_m, G_list = mean_and_tangents(masters rotations from u)\n   A = lever(theta_m, r_0)\n   v = A.T @ f_b + m_b   # mean-chart slave moment covector\n   lin_m = m_b - skew(r_0).T @ f_b  # = m_b + skew(r_0) f_b? skew^T = -skew, so -skew(r_0)^T f_b = +skew(r_0) f_b... \n   ```\n   Let me recompute: term per master i: G_i^T v + w_i (skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b).\n   \n   Check direct limit (single master, G=I, w=1): term = v + skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b = A^T f_b + m_b - skew(r_0) f_b... skew(r_0)^T = -skew(r_0). So = A^T f_b - skew(r_0) f_b + m_b - m_b = (A - skew(r_0))^T f_b? A^T f_b - skew(r_0)^T f_b = (A - skew(r_0)^T... hmm: A^T f_b + skew(r_0)^T f_b = (A + skew(r_0)^T)^T? No: (A + skew(r_0))^T f_b = A^T f_b + skew(r_0)^T f_b. So direct term = (A + skew(r_0))^T f_b = delta_A^T f_b. And old code put delta_A^T f_b at slave_theta → theta_proj^T delta_A^T f_b = P_1^T delta_A^T f_b (w=1). New: P_1^T [delta_A^T f_b]. Same. \n\n   And identical-rotation limit: G_i = w_i I (mean of identical rotations: theta_m = theta_common; perturbing each theta_i identically... hmm, actually G_i for identical rotations: the mean function theta_m(theta_1..theta_n) with all theta_i equal gives theta_m = theta_1; dtheta_m/dtheta_i = w_i I? Let's verify with the Karcher mean: R_m = mean with weights w. If all R_i = R, then R_m = R. Perturb theta_i → theta_i + dtheta_i. The mean satisfies sum_i w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) = 0. Linearizing: R_m = exp(theta + dtheta_m)... For small perturbations around common theta: Log(R_m^T R_i) ≈ J (dtheta_i - dtheta_m) where J = J_l at... roughly, sum w_i (dtheta_i - dtheta_m) = 0 → dtheta_m = sum w_i dtheta_i. So G_i = w_i I. Yes.)\n\n   Then term_i = w_i v + w_i (skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b) = w_i (A^T f_b + m_b + skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b) = w_i (A + skew(r_0))^T f_b = w_i delta_A^T f_b. Sum over i: P^T... each master gets its own P_i^T w_i delta_A^T f_b; total = theta_proj^T delta_A^T f_b. Same as old. Legacy retained.\n\n5. **geometric_tangent**: needs second derivatives of the mean and slave orientation contracted with m_b, plus offset translation derivatives contracted with f_b. The current force-only lever Hessian assumes theta_bar linear. New geometric tangent:\n\n   K_geom = d/dq [ (delta_T(q))^T f(q) ]... Actually the geometric tangent arises from d(J^T F)/dq where F is held frozen: K_geom = sum over entries of d/dq [delta_T(q)^T f_b] with f frozen = (d delta_T^T f_b / dq). This is the Hessian of the constraint potential.\n\n   For the new map: g(q) = delta_T(q)^T f = per entry:\n   - translation: (A(theta_m) dtheta_m/dq + skew(r_0) dtheta_lin/dq)^T f_b\n   - rotation: (dtheta_m/dq - dtheta_lin/dq)^T m_b\n\n   The geometric tangent is dg/dq — a full n_red×n_red Hessian involving second derivatives of theta_m wrt (theta_1,...,theta_n) and of A. The old code approximated this by H_b(theta_bar) contracted with theta_proj only (i.e., assuming dtheta_bar/dq = theta_proj and ignoring second derivative of theta_bar wrt theta_i, which is zero for linear average — for the linear average, the only second derivative comes from A(theta_bar), giving theta_proj^T H theta_proj).\n\n   For the new map, second derivatives of the mean are nonzero. Full implementation: \n   K_geom_entry = sum_{i,j} P_i^T [ d/dtheta_j (G_i^T v) ] P_j + ... where v = A^T f_b + m_b, plus the w_i linear terms (constant, no second derivative): d/dq of sum_i P_i^T w_i(skew^T f_b - m_b) = 0 since it's constant in q. So:\n\n   K_geom = sum_{i,j} P_i^T [ d(G_i^T v)/dtheta_j ] P_j.\n\n   where d(G_i^T v)/dtheta_j is a 3×3 block: derivative wrt master j's rotation vector of the covector G_i^T v(theta_1..theta_n). Note v depends on theta_m and m_b, f_b frozen.\n\n   This is the \"mean Hessian\" term. Computing this analytically is heavy. Options: finite differences of the map derivative — the review says \"include second derivatives of the mean itself and the slave orientation, contracted with the complete slave rotation imbalance m_b, as well as offset translation derivatives contracted with f_b.\"\n\n   A robust approach: compute K_geom by finite-differencing the reduced force correction g(q) = delta_T(q)^T f (the per-entry contribution), i.e., K_geom ≈ [g(q+h e_k) - g(q-h e_k)]/(2h) over the master theta dof columns. But g has support only on master theta dofs (columns of P_i), so FD over the union of master theta dofs of the entry — at most 3*n_masters columns. Each evaluation of g requires recomputing the mean and G_i — cheap (3×3 ops). n_masters is 2 in the EP fixture. So 6 columns × 2 evals = 12 mean evaluations per entry per geometric tangent call. That's acceptable.\n\n   Actually wait — is FD acceptable \"quality\"? The old H_b itself was FD (rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian uses central differences with h=1e-6). So FD is consistent with existing style. But better: FD the exact g function including all terms (mean second derivatives + orientation m_b terms + lever f_b terms). That automatically includes everything the review demands. And it exactly matches the forward map, so J^T consistency holds.\n\n   But careful: geometric_tangent must be symmetric; symmetrize.\n\n   Alternatively, derive analytically: too error-prone under time budget. FD of g is a \"complete derivative\" and discriminating tests (FD of whole forward map, reduced net force) will validate. The review demands inclusion of the terms; FD of the true g includes them exactly (to FD accuracy). I'll implement FD with the same h=1e-6 style, but note: g involves theta_m via Log/Exp — smooth. FD error ~1e-6ish relative — the old H_b had the same character.\n\n   Hmm, but one concern: geometric tangent enters the Newton tangent; FD noise ~1e-6*h... The old code already used FD h=1e-6 central differences → error O(h^2)=1e-12 absolute. Fine.\n\n   Implementation of geometric_tangent (new):\n   ```\n   for entry:\n     f_b, m_b from imbalance\n     if both tiny: skip\n     define g(theta_list) -> covector contributions on master theta dofs:\n        compute theta_m, G_i; A; v = A^T f_b + m_b\n        g_i = G_i^T v + w_i (skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b)  (constant part included; its derivative is zero but including it is harmless and keeps g == exact delta_T^T f)\n     K_entry = sum_{i,j} P_i^T dGcov/dtheta_j P_j via FD over master theta columns.\n   ```\n   FD: for each master j and each component c of theta_j: perturb u[m_theta_j][c] by ±h, recompute all g_i, then dg/dc = (g_i(+) - g_i(-))/(2h) for each i. Then K += P_i^T (dg_i/dtheta_jc) P_j[:,c]... Since P_j e_c picks column... Let me structure: K_geom = sum_j sum_c [dg/dtheta_jc] where dg/dtheta_jc is a covector in q-space: dg/dtheta_jc = sum_i P_i^T (dg_i/dtheta_jc). And dg/dtheta_jc as function of q: g(q) depends on q only through master thetas: theta_i = P_i q. So d g(q)/d q = sum_j (dg/dtheta_j) P_j. So K_geom = sum_j [sum_i P_i^T Dg_i/Dtheta_j] P_j where Dg_i/Dtheta_j is 3×3.\n\n   With FD: for j in masters, for c in 0..2: u± → g_i± (vector of 3 per master i). Dg_i/Dtheta_j[:,c] = (g_i+ - g_i-)/(2h). Then block_ij = 3×3 matrix whose column c is that. K += P_i^T block_ij P_j.\n\n   Cost: per entry, 2*3*n_masters mean computations, each cheap. Sparse products P_i^T block P_j: P_i is 3×n_red sparse with ~3-9 nnz. Fine.\n\n   Also need to keep the old path for... no — the new map replaces the old on the active route entirely. But direct entries (single master) also go through the same code; for them the mean is exact identity and G=I, and the FD reduces to the old H_b behavior. But the old code used the analytic-ish FD of A^T f_b only (H_b). The new FD includes m_b terms — for direct, the rotation correction is zero (M_proj - theta_proj = 0), so g_i = delta_A^T f_b + w(skew^T f_b - m_b); the constant terms have zero derivative; derivative of delta_A^T f_b wrt theta_1 = old H_b. Consistent.\n\n   Hmm wait — actually for direct entries, does the old geometric tangent include m_b? Old: H_b = d(A^T f_b)/dtheta, contracted theta_proj^T H theta_proj. New: d(delta_A^T f_b)/dtheta = same H_b (since skew term constant). Plus zero from m_b. Same. Good.\n\n6. **transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters**: distribute w_i f_b to master translations and G_i^T(m_b + A^T f_b) to master rotation covectors. Per review: \"transfer w_i*f_b to master translations and G_i.T*(m_b+A.T*f_b) to master rotation covectors, then zero slave terms.\"\n\n   Note: old transferred w_i*(m_b + A^T f_b). New: G_i^T*(m_b + A^T f_b). For direct: G^T = I, w=1 → same. For identical: G_i^T = w_i I → same as old. \n\n   Wait, but should the linear-part correction terms (skew^T f_b - m_b)*w_i appear here? No — transfer is for support reactions: the physical slave wrench (f_b, m_b) is distributed to masters as the static equivalent. The review says exactly: w_i f_b to translations, G_i^T(m_b + A^T f_b) to rotations. Hmm, but is that the physically correct transfer? The transfer should be the transpose of the kinematics relating slave wrench to master wrenches... Actually for reactions: the slave is eliminated; its imbalance wrench must be carried by masters. The kinematic map: u_b = sum w_i u_i + (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0, theta_b = theta_m. The virtual work: f_b·du_b + m_b·dtheta_b = sum_i [w_i f_b]·du_i + [G_i^T(A^T f_b + m_b)]·dtheta_i... let me verify: du_b = sum w_i du_i + A dtheta_m + skew(r_0) dtheta_lin = sum w_i du_i + sum_i A G_i dtheta_i + sum_i w_i skew(r_0) dtheta_i. dtheta_b = dtheta_m = sum_i G_i dtheta_i.\n\n   f_b·du_b + m_b·dtheta_b = sum_i w_i f_b·du_i + sum_i [G_i^T A^T f_b + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b + G_i^T m_b]·dtheta_i.\n\n   So the exact master rotation covector is G_i^T(A^T f_b + m_b) + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b. Hmm! The review says G_i^T(m_b + A^T f_b) — it omits the w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b term. But wait — the reaction transfer is about physical wrench at masters. The T_linear already includes the linear lever: the linear constraint u_b,lin = sum w_i u_i - skew(r_0) theta_bar means the linear transfer already moves -skew(r_0)^T... hmm.\n\n   Actually, transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters operates on the FULL physical imbalance vector, distributing slave entries to master entries. The old code: moment_covector = m_b + A^T f_b, then w_i * moment_covector to master thetas, w_i f_b to master translations. Note: old transfer did NOT include the linear lever term because the linear part (theta_bar × r_0) is part of T_linear's slave_u row — i.e., in the full-coordinate picture, the slave_u row of the linear constraint already references master thetas. Hmm, no — transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters works on the full physical vector where the slave dofs hold the beam node's own imbalance. The masters receive the slave's wrench. The old transfer: w_i f_b (force) and w_i(m_b + A^T f_b) (moment covector). The A^T f_b term accounts for the offset lever: moving force f_b from the slave node to the weighted attachment point... \n\n   For the reaction oracle, what matters is that total physical wrench is preserved. The review's exact identity: Delta_M = D^T (m_b + A^T f_b) for the OLD map, where D = sum w_i J_i^{-1} - J_b^{-1}. The corrected transfer per review: G_i^T (m_b + A^T f_b). Let me trust the review's formula: transfer w_i f_b to translations and G_i^T(m_b + A^T f_b) to rotations.\n\n   Hmm, but wait — there's an inconsistency with my virtual-work derivation which gave an extra w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b on master rotations. Let me recheck. The virtual work transfer (J^T f) is reduce_force, which I derived: per master i: G_i^T(A^T f_b + m_b) + w_i(skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b). The extra terms -w_i m_b + w_i skew^T f_b come from subtracting the linear T_linear contribution (T_linear^T f already includes theta_proj^T m_b = sum w_i P_i^T m_b and the linear lever -theta_proj^T skew(r_0)^T f_b... wait sign).\n\n   Let me recompute T_linear^T f's master-theta content: slave_theta rows of T_linear = theta_proj (theta_b = sum w_i theta_i). slave_u rows = sum w_i e_{u_i} - skew(r_0) theta_proj (u_b = sum w_i u_i + theta_bar × r_0, and theta_bar × r_0 = -skew(r_0) theta_bar). So T_linear^T f includes: from slave_theta: theta_proj^T m_b = sum_i P_i^T w_i m_b; from slave_u: -theta_proj^T skew(r_0)^T f_b = -sum_i P_i^T w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b.\n\n   Total J^T f (new) = T_linear^T f + delta_T^T f where delta_T^T f = sum_i P_i^T [G_i^T v + w_i(skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b)], v = A^T f_b + m_b.\n\n   Sum: master i rotation covector in J^T f = P_i^T [w_i m_b - w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b + G_i^T v + w_i skew(r_0)^T f_b - w_i m_b] = P_i^T G_i^T v. \n\n   So the FULL J^T f gives master rotation covector G_i^T (A^T f_b + m_b) — the linear terms cancel exactly. That's the review's transfer formula. The transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters, however, is not J^T f — it's a full-vector redistribution for reaction computation: it replaces slave entries with master entries such that... the total physical wrench is preserved. Old code: w_i f_b + w_i(m_b + A^T f_b). For the new map, the review says G_i^T(m_b + A^T f_b) for rotations.\n\n   But hold on — is the transfer supposed to be the adjoint of the kinematics from masters to slave (i.e., J^T)? The reaction oracle computes support reactions from the transferred imbalance. The physical requirement: the transferred master wrenches + supports + applied loads balance. The slave's imbalance wrench (f_b, m_b) is a physical wrench at the slave node. The masters physically hold the slave through the constraint. The static equivalence: the constraint forces on masters must be such that their total wrench equals the slave wrench transferred... \n\n   The old transfer: put w_i f_b at each master translation and w_i(m_b + A^T f_b) at each master rotation. Sum of forces = f_b (weights sum 1). Total moment about origin: sum_i [x_i × w_i f_b] + sum_i w_i J_l(theta_i)^{-T}(m_b + A^T f_b)... when converted to spatial moments. The review derived Delta_M = D^T v for this transfer, showing it's NOT wrench-preserving unless D=0. With G_i^T v instead of w_i v: the review's obligations say the corrected transfer is G_i^T(m_b + A^T f_b). Presumably then Delta_M = 0 identically (the equivariance identity sum G_i J_i^{-1} = J_m^{-1} ensures wrench preservation). I'll trust the review.\n\n   So new transfer: for each master: imbalance[m_u] += w_i f_b; imbalance[m_theta] += G_i^T (m_b + A^T f_b). Zero slave terms.\n\n   Check direct: G=I → m_b + A^T f_b, w=1 → old behavior. Identical rotations: G_i^T = w_i I → old behavior. \n\n7. **apply_kinematics / to_linear_displacement**: \n   - apply: u[slave_u] += (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0; u[slave_theta] += theta_m - theta_lin.\n   - to_linear (inverse): u[slave_u] -= (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0; u[slave_theta] -= (theta_m - theta_lin). Note the review: \"subtract these same master-dependent corrections from the supplied physical slave translation AND rotation. Do not overwrite an incompatible supplied slave rotation with theta_lin.\" Since the correction depends only on master rotations (which are unchanged by the slave correction — masters are distinct dofs), the inverse is exact. But careful: if entry masters include dofs modified by another entry's correction (chained), iteration might be needed. Old code had the same single-pass structure; keep single-pass per entry (entries processed sequentially; each entry's correction depends on its masters' rotations — if a master is another entry's slave, the order matters). Old code had this same hazard; keep the same sequential semantics but note both apply and inverse use master rotations from the CURRENT working vector. Hmm — in apply_kinematics, u[slave_u] is modified; masters of the same entry are shell dofs, not modified. For safety, compute all corrections first from the input state, then apply — that makes apply a proper function of input. Old code computed theta_bar from u as it goes (entry by entry). If entry A's slave theta is entry B's master theta... slave theta gets modified (+theta_m - theta_lin) in the new map! Old map didn't modify slave theta at all. New map modifies slave rotations — so if a master of B is a slave of A, order matters. To be deterministic: precompute all entries' corrections from the input u, then apply all. I'll do that (two-pass). Same for inverse.\n\n   Wait, actually there's a subtlety in to_linear_displacement: the input is the physical nonlinear u; masters' rotations in the physical state are the same as in linear coordinates (master dofs are not corrected — corrections apply only to slave dofs). So computing corrections from the input physical u directly is exact. Good. And apply_kinematics: input is linear u; master rotations in linear coordinates = physical master rotations (masters unmodified). Good. So single evaluation from input is exact, no iteration needed, as long as masters are never slaves. If a master were a slave of another entry, its rotation would be modified — precompute-from-input avoids order dependence and is the mathematically clean definition. I'll precompute.\n\n8. **The mean