si = J_l(psi)^{-1} dphi_s? Or J_r? Let me recall: exp(psi + δ) ≈ exp(psi) + J_r(psi) δ (right Jacobian: derivative of exp wrt its own argument). Also exp(psi + δ) = exp(J_l(psi)... \n\n   Known identities:\n   - d exp(psi)/d psi · δ = J_r(psi) δ exp(psi)... in the sense exp(psi ⊕ δ) = exp(δ') exp(psi) with δ' = J_l(psi) δ. Specifically: exp(psi+δ) = exp(J_l(psi) δ) exp(psi) = exp(psi) exp(J_r(psi) δ). Hmm, I need to be careful. The standard BCH-based identities:\n     exp(psi + δ) = exp(psi) exp(J_r(psi)^{-1}... \n\n   Let me just define via small perturbations:\n   exp(psi + δ) = exp(psi) exp(ε_r) where ε_r = J_r(psi)^{-1}... no.\n\n   The right Jacobian J_r satisfies: exp(psi + δ) = exp(psi) exp(J_r(psi)^{-1} δ)? Let me verify at psi=0: J_r(0)=I, exp(δ)=exp(0)exp(δ). OK. General: known identity d exp(psi)/d psi = exp(psi) J_r(psi)^{-1}... hmm I recall: exp(psi + δ) ≈ exp(psi) exp(J_r(psi)^{-1} δ)? Or with J_l: exp(psi+δ) ≈ exp(J_l(psi) δ) exp(psi).\n\n   Both are standard: \n   - exp(psi + δ) = exp(J_l(psi) δ) exp(psi) + O(δ²)  (left form)\n   - exp(psi + δ) = exp(psi) exp(J_r(psi) δ) + O(δ²)  (right form)\n   Hmm, which one has J and which J^{-1}? At psi=0 both J=I so can't distinguish. Let me derive with BCH: exp(a)exp(b) = exp(a + b + ½a×b + ...). Take exp(psi+δ) = exp(psi) exp(ε): psi + δ = psi + ε + ½ psi×ε + ... → δ = ε + ½ psi×ε + ... = (I + ½ skew(psi)) ε + ... So ε = (I - ½ skew(psi)) δ + ... = J_r(psi)^{-1} δ where J_r(psi) = I - ½ skew(psi) + ... Hmm: J_r(psi) = I - (1-cos)/θ² skew + ... The standard right Jacobian: J_r(θ) = I - (1-cos θ)/θ² [θ×] + (θ - sin θ)/θ³ [θ×]². And identity: exp(θ + δθ) = exp(θ) exp(J_r(θ) δθ)? Let me check first order: J_r(θ) ≈ I - ½[θ×]. From BCH above: ε = (I - ½ skew(psi)) δ → ε = J_r δ with J_r ≈ I - ½ skew. Yes! So exp(psi + δ) = exp(psi) exp(J_r(psi) δ). Similarly exp(psi + δ) = exp(J_l(psi) δ) exp(psi) with J_l(θ) = I + ½[θ×] + ...\n\n   OK so with the repo's `_rotation_right_jacobian` presumably J_r. Let me check the repo definitions later.\n\n   Now the mean condition and its differential. Use the spatial form: mean satisfies m(theta_m) = sum_i w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) = 0? Wait — Karcher mean on SO(3) with left-invariant metric: sum_i w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) = 0 (geodesic from mean to each R_i in the mean's local frame). Under left multiplication by Q: R_i → Q R_i, R_m → Q R_m: (Q R_m)^T (Q R_i) = R_m^T R_i — invariant. So the condition is left-equivariant: if R_m is the mean of R_i, then Q R_m is the mean of Q R_i. \n\n   Fixed-point iteration: given current theta_m, compute f = sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) (a \"body\" vector), update R_m ← R_m exp(f)? or exp(f) R_m? Since f is expressed in... Log(R_m^T R_i) is the rotation vector of R_m^T R_i; R_m exp(f) = R_m R_i-ish direction. Standard barycentric: R_m ← exp( sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) ) R_m — that uses spatial f' = R_m f. Both converge to the same mean; the standard is R_m ← exp(f) R_m with f = sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i)? Hmm: exp(f) R_m where f = sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i): check fixed point: f=0. Convergence: this is the standard gradient descent/Newton on SO(3). Actually the common form (e.g., in \"Averaging rotations\": R ← R exp( sum w_i Log(R^T R_i) ))? Let me think: Log(R^T R_i) is the body-frame geodesic error. The update R ← R exp(e) moves R toward R_i in body frame. Or R ← exp(R e) R = R exp(e) R^T R... exp(Re)R = R exp(e) (since exp(R e) = R exp(e) R^T). Oh nice: exp(R_m f) R_m = R_m exp(f). So both forms coincide?? exp(spatial)·R = R·exp(body) when spatial = R·body. And Log(R^T R_i) is body; R·Log(R^T R_i) is the spatial version. So R ← exp(R_m f) R_m = R_m exp(f). Same thing. Good — one iteration form.\n\n   Convergence: quadratic near solution. Iterate until ||f|| < tol (1e-12) with max iterations (~50) and convergence safeguard. Refusal: if not converged, or if any ||Log(R_m^T R_i)|| >= pi (outside injectivity radius), raise ValueError (explicit refusal on ambiguity/chart cases). Also weights: existing validator admits mixed-sign weights summing to 1; the Karcher mean with negative weights may be nonunique/singular — the review says handle admitted weights or explicitly refuse. The adoption note says \"nonnegative normalized existing weights and explicit refusal on unsupported ambiguity/chart/weight cases\". So: refuse if any w_i < 0 or sum != 1 (within tolerance). Standard EP weights are positive. But wait — \"Retain direct/zero-offset legacy behavior\": direct = single master w=1. Zero-offset legacy: entries with r_0 = 0? Hmm, \"zero-offset legacy\" refers to descriptors with zero eccentricity — currently inactive. The adopted contract: new mean applies to the ACTIVE rotating-offset route. For entries with zero eccentricity... the mean still changes slave orientation. Hmm — \"Do not expand zero-offset legacy capability\" and \"Zero-offset descriptors are currently inactive\". The active route = entries in the map (which have eccentricity). I'll apply the mean to all entries of the map uniformly (the map is only built for rotating-arm descriptors). Actually wait — should entries with ||r_0|| ≈ 0 keep the old orientation? The old map sets theta_b = theta_bar regardless of r_0. The adopted contract says weighted deformed attachment point AND orientation must transform equivariantly — orientation included. So apply mean to all active entries. But then \"zero-offset legacy\" — if a zero-offset descriptor were admitted, its orientation would now use the mean too. The review says zero-offset descriptors are currently inactive, so no behavior change. Fine — uniform treatment.\n\n   Hmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider: \"Retain direct/zero-offset legacy behavior\" from the user message. Direct = single master: mean reduces exactly to theta_1 → identical behavior. Zero-offset: r_0=0 → translation correction zero; orientation now via mean. If zero-offset descriptors are inactive, no observable change. But to be careful about \"legacy behavior\": maybe zero-offset entries currently produce theta_b = thet