{"id": "reasoning-e29781d2-0140-52a4-a5fa-bef4f103ab48-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350228159, "updatedAt": 1792350228159, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "All utilities exist. `_nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta)` = R(theta) - I - skew(theta). So old apply: u[slave_u] += (R(theta_bar) - I - skew(theta_bar)) @ r_0. Since linear T contains skew(theta_bar) r_0 = -skew(r_0) theta_bar... wait: linear lever is theta_bar × r_0 = skew(theta_bar) r_0. T_linear slave_u row: sum w_i u_i + skew(theta_bar) r_0. Correction to reach exact: (R-I) r_0 - skew(theta_bar) r_0 = (R - I - skew(theta_bar)) r_0. ✓ matches.\n\nNew apply: correction = (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0 = [(R_m - I - skew(theta_m)) + skew(theta_m) - skew(theta_lin)] r_0 = _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_m) @ r_0 + skew(theta_m - theta_lin) r_0.\n\nSo: u[slave_u] += _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_m) @ r_0 + skew(theta_m - theta_lin) @ r_0... wait skew(theta_m - theta_lin) r_0 — careful: skew(theta_m) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0 = skew(theta_m - theta_lin) r_0. ✓.\n\nAnd u[slave_theta] += (theta_m - theta_lin).\n\nDirect limit: theta_m = theta_lin → second term zero, first term = old. ✓ Identical rotations: same. ✓\n\nNow, plan the implementation in corotational.py:\n\n1. New module-level function `_so3_weighted_mean(master_thetas, weights)` returning (theta_m, G_list). With:\n   - weights validation (nonnegative, sum 1 within 1e-8) — raise ValueError.\n   - single master: exact.\n   - all-equal: exact.\n   - fixed-point: theta_m ← theta_lin start; iterate f = sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i); update theta_m ← Log(exp(f) R_m)... in rotation-vector form: R_new = exp(f) R_m → theta_new = rotation_vector_from_matrix(rotation_matrix_from_vector(f) @ R_m). Convergence: ||f|| < 1e-14 (or max 100 iters). Refusal if any ||psi_i|| >= pi - 1e-6 at convergence, or non-convergence, or B singular.\n   - G_i = R_m B^{-1} w_i A_i R_m^T, A_i = _rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(psi_i), B = sum w_i A_i.\n\n   Wait — check the G_i formula sign conventions once more. From my derivation: G_i = R_m B^{-1} w_i A_i R_m^T where A_i = J_l(psi_i)^{-1}. Hmm wait, let me recheck: I had A_i = J_l(psi_i)^{-1} and derived:\n\n   B R_m^T d = sum_i w_i A_i R_m^T e_i, B = sum w_i A_i.\n   → R_m^T d = B^{-1} sum w_i A_i R_m^T e_i\n   → d = R_m B^{-1} sum_i w_i A_i R_m^T e_i\n   → G_i = R_m B^{-1} w_i A_i R_m^T. ✓\n\n   Sanity check single master: psi_1 = 0, A_1 = I, B = I (w=1), G_1 = R_m I I R_m^T = I. ✓ (theta_m = theta_1, dtheta_m = dtheta_1.)\n\n   Sanity check equivariance identity numerically in tests.\n\n   One more check — the fixed-point iteration convergence from theta_lin: standard; for masters within a convex neighborhood it converges. Add safeguard: if ||f|| > previous ||f|| and not decreasing over several iters, or ||f|| > pi, refuse.\n\n2. Entry changes:\n   - __init__: accept `master_theta_projs` (tuple of csr 3×n_red per master). Keep theta_proj.\n   - `master_rotations(u)`: list of theta_i.\n   - `mean_state(u)`: returns (theta_lin, theta_m, G_list, weights).\n   - `master_rotation` stays (theta_lin) for compatibility? It's used in the map methods; I'll refactor internally.\n\n3. Map method changes:\n   - apply_kinematics: two-pass (precompute corrections from input, then apply).\n   - to_linear_displacement: two-pass, subtract corrections (translation AND rotation).\n   - jacobian: build slave_u block: A(theta_m) M_proj + skew(r_0) theta_proj; slave_theta block: M_proj - theta_proj. Where M_proj = sum_i G_i P_i (build as sparse: for each master, dense 3×3 @ sparse 3×n_red → sparse).\n   - reduce_force: f_mod approach: per entry, per master: f_mod[m_theta_i] += G_i^T v + w_i (skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b), where v = A(theta_m)^T f_b + m_b. Wait — from my derivation: delta_T^T f = sum_i P_i^T [G_i^T v + w_i(skew(r_0)^T f_b - m_b)]. And T_linear^T f_mod with f_mod[m_theta_i] += that bracket gives P_i^T bracket. ✓\n\n     Hmm wait, but there's a subtlety: reduce_force's f_mod trick requires that the master theta dofs' T_linear rows are exactly P_i = dtheta_i/dq. True by definition of T_linear. ✓\n\n     But CAREFUL: what if a master theta dof coincides with a slave u dof of the same or another entry? Slave u dofs are beam translations; master thetas are shell rotations — different dofs. In weird chained configs, could collide across entries; the += accumulation handles it correctly since contributions are additive.\n\n     Edge: what if m_theta dofs overlap with slave_theta dofs of the same entry? Not physically meaningful; ignore.\n\n   - geometric_tangent: FD of covector function as derived. Note: must skip when both f_b and m_b tiny. Also when theta_proj and P_i nnz == 0 skip.\n   - transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters: per master: imbalance[m_u] += w_i f_b; imbalance[m_theta] += G_i^T (m_b + A^T f_b). Zero slave.\n   - reduce_tangent: unchanged (calls jacobian/geometric_tangent).\n\n4. build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map: compute master_theta_projs per entry; pass down. Also extend the downstream guard to slave rotations of multi-master entries.\n\n5. Downstream guard: extend `_reject_downstream_rotating_slave_translation_dependencies` to also collect slave_theta_dofs of entries with len(masters) > 1 and refuse equations using them as masters. Update docstring.\n\nNow — what about `has_nonlinear_rotating_mpc` and other seams? Fine.\n\nWhat about places that assume slave theta = theta_bar? E.g., reactions computation, postcheck. Let me check the postcheck and reaction code paths that use the map. Also `full_u` (R5 DC admission) at line 4026/4105ish. Let me look at how constraint_postcheck works with the map — grep \"constraint_postcheck\" and check whether it verifies slave theta against linear relation.", "summary": []}
