{"id": "reasoning-246e9711-ceee-52a8-822d-c31c8aaaaadf-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350259357, "updatedAt": 1792350259357, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "CRLF as well. I'll do the corotational.py edits with a Python script using exact anchors. Let me design the full new code.\n\n### New function: `_so3_weighted_mean`\n\n```python\ndef _so3_weighted_mean(\n    master_thetas: Sequence[np.ndarray],\n    weights: Sequence[float],\n    label: str,\n) -> Tuple[np.ndarray, Tuple[np.ndarray, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"Intrinsic weighted SO(3) mean with its exact tangent derivatives.\n\n    Returns ``(theta_m, (G_i, ...))`` where ``R(theta_m)`` is the Karcher-type\n    weighted mean of the master rotations and ``G_i = dtheta_m/dtheta_i``.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\n    thetas = [np.asarray(t, dtype=float).reshape(3) for t in master_thetas]\n    ws = [float(w) for w in weights]\n    if len(thetas) == 0: raise ValueError\n    if any(w < 0.0 for w in ws):\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: intrinsic SO(3) mean requires nonnegative weights; got {ws}\")\n    wsum = sum(ws)\n    if abs(wsum - 1.0) > 1.0e-8:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: intrinsic SO(3) mean requires weights forming a partition of unity; sum={wsum}\")\n    if len(thetas) == 1:\n        return thetas[0].copy(), (np.eye(3),)\n    # exact identical-rotation shortcut\n    first = thetas[0]\n    if all(np.array_equal(t, first) for t in thetas[1:]):\n        return first.copy(), tuple(w * np.eye(3) for w in ws)\n    # fixed-point iteration\n    theta_m = sum(w*t for ...)  # linear average start\n    R_m = rotation_matrix_from_vector(theta_m)\n    for _ in range(100):\n        f = np.zeros(3)\n        psis = []\n        for t, w in zip(thetas, ws):\n            psi = rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m.T @ rotation_matrix_from_vector(t))\n            psis.append(psi)\n            f += w * psi\n        if norm(f) < 1e-14: break\n        R_m = rotation_matrix_from_vector(f) @ R_m\n    else: raise ValueError(not converged)\n    theta_m = rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m)\n    # chart refusal\n    for psi in psis: if norm(psi) >= pi*(1-1e-6): raise ValueError\n    # tangents\n    A = [ _rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(psi) for psi in psis ]\n    B = sum(w*a)\n    if abs(det(B)) < 1e-10: raise ValueError\n    Binv = np.linalg.inv(B)  # solve instead\n    G = [ R_m @ (Binv @ (w * Ai)) @ R_m.T ...]\n```\n\nWait — G_i = R_m B^{-1} w_i A_i R_m^T. Careful about the order: G_i = R_m @ Binv @ (w_i * A_i) @ R_m.T.\n\nHmm wait, actually — one issue: the fixed-point update `R_m ← exp(f) R_m` — is f the spatial or body increment? f = sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) is in the BODY frame of R_m (since R_m^T R_i). The update R_m exp(f) moves toward R_i in body frame. exp(f) R_m would be a spatial increment. Which is correct for the Karcher mean?\n\nThe Karcher mean condition (left-invariant metric): sum_i w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) = 0 — this is the body-frame geodesic condition. Gradient descent: R_m ← R_m exp(epsilon * f)? Hmm: moving R_m along the geodesic toward R_i: R_m(t) = R_m exp(t * Log(R_m^T R_i)). So the update R_m ← R_m exp(f) is the natural one (body frame). \n\nBut wait — does the fixed point of R_m ← R_m exp(f) converge to the mean? This is the standard \"barycentric\" iteration usually written as R ← R exp(sum w_i Log(R^T R_i)) — yes, e.g., in Hartley et al. \"Rotation Averaging\" the intrinsic iteration is R ← R exp(sum w_i log(R^T R_i)). Good.\n\nNote: exp(f) R_m ≠ R_m exp(f) in general. I must use R_m exp(f). Let me redo the differential derivation to make sure it matches this condition — my derivation used the condition c(theta_m) = sum w_i Log(R_m^T R_i) = 0 and perturbed R_m → exp(d) R_m (spatial). The differential result stands regardless of the iteration used; the condition defines the mean. ✓\n\nBut wait, I should do