ration typically converges quadratically to ~1e-16. The threshold is an upper bound; actual residual usually far below. But not guaranteed ≤1e-16. Risk: FD noise up to 5e-8 vs atol 1e-10 → flaky.\n\nMitigation: tighten the mean convergence threshold to machine level: iterate until ||f|| <= 1e-16 or no improvement... Actually the fixed-point at convergence: f ~ O(delta²) where delta is current error; final ||f|| can hit exactly 0.0 or ~1e-17. Let me set threshold at 0.0? Loop: compute f; if ||f|| == 0.0 break... might not reach exactly 0. Alternative: threshold 1e-15 and also break when ||f|| stops decreasing (converged to machine). Then residual ≤ ~1e-16 typically. FD noise ~1e-16/2e-7 = 5e-10 — still marginal vs atol 1e-10.\n\nHmm. But wait — the FD in the test uses full_u(q±dq) — the mean is computed from master thetas which vary analytically with q; the mean function is smooth; its iteration converges to the true mean each time. The \"noise\" is the difference between the converged iterate and the true mean — deterministic, smooth in q? Not exactly smooth: the number of iterations may vary with q, causing tiny discontinuities ~convergence tolerance. That's the FD noise source.\n\nBetter: make the mean converge to machine precision: threshold ||f|| <= 1e-15, plus a stagnation break (if ||f|| >= prev_norm, break — converged to machine). With quadratic convergence, final ||f|| ~ 1e-16-1e-17 or exactly 0. The FD noise then ~1e-16/2e-7 = 5e-10... still above 1e-10 atol? Hmm, but actually if f converges to exactly 0.0 (common: the update hits the fixed point exactly in floating point), noise is 0.\n\nAlternatively relax the test tolerance for interp? The test is parametrized over both routes with the same tolerances. The review demands FD coverage of the whole forward map — tolerances are my choice in NEW tests, but this EXISTING test has rtol 1e-8/atol 1e-10 for J. I could adjust atol for the interp route... but weakening existing tolerances is suspect. Let me think a