e magnitude more carefully. theta_m discontinuity ~1e-16 rad. du/dq column: FD = (u(q+h)-u(q-h))/2h; the discontinuity contributes 1e-16/2e-7 = 5e-10 to ONE entry of the column (the slave theta rows). atol 1e-10 → fails if it happens.\n\nHmm, but does the discontinuity actually hit? The iteration count changes only when thetas cross threshold boundaries — measure-zero-ish but FD probes specific points; the two evaluations (q+h, q-h) might land on different iteration counts with error difference up to ~2e-16... \n\nMore robust: make the iteration run a FIXED number of iterations once converged (e.g., keep iterating until nf <= 1e-16 or stagnation, then run 2 more updates)? No — extra updates at machine level just round.\n\nCleanest robust fix: after the fixed-point converges, do ONE Newton-polish using the exact differential: solve for the exact root via the IFT: theta_m_final = theta_m - B_param^{-1} c where c = sum w psi_i in parameter coordinates... Actually the condition c(theta_m)=0 with dc/dtheta_m = -R_m... we have the exact differential machinery. Newton polish: given current theta_m with gradient f (body frame), the parameter Newton step: theta_m_new = theta_m + J_l(theta_m)^{-1} R_m^T... hmm wait. f is in body frame: R_m exp(f) is the update. The parameter increment: theta_m' = Log(R_m exp(f)). Compute directly: theta_m' = rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m @ exp(f)). That's what the iteration does (via matrix). The quadratic convergence means the last step has error e_last ~ (e_prev)². If e_prev ~ 1e-8, e_last ~ 1e-16 — one more step: e ~ 1e-32 → 0 in float. So iterating 2 extra times past the threshold drives to exact 0 typically. \n\nSimplest: set threshold to 0.0 with stagnation detection:\n```\nnf_prev = None\nconverged = False\nfor _ in range(100):\n    psis...; f...; nf = norm(f)\n    if nf == 0.0: converged = True; break\n    if nf >= np.pi: break\n    if nf_prev is not None and nf >= nf_prev:\n        converged = nf_prev <= 1e-12  # stagnated at machine precision\n        break\n    nf_prev = nf\n    R_m = R_m @ exp(f)\n```\nWait — subtle: if nf stagnates (nf >= nf_prev) we break WITHOUT applying the last update; the current R_m has gradient nf (machine level). Accept if machine-level. The fixed point in floating point: R_m @ exp(f) == R_m when f ~ 1e-17 (exp(f) ≈ I + f, and R_m(I+f) rounds to R_m if |R_m f| < ulp(R_m) ~ 1e-16·|R_m| — f ~ 1e-17 gives R_m f ~ 1e-17 < ulp/2... borderline). In practice it converges to nf == 0.0 exactly often.\n\nOK — implement stagnation-accept, then empirically check the J-FD test. If it fails at 1e-10, I'll evaluate then. Actually you know what — there's an even better approach for determinism: WOLFE-style — no. Let me also reconsider: maybe use np.linalg solve-based Newton on the rotation vector directly:\n\nNewton on c(theta_m) = sum_i w_i psi_i(theta_m) = 0 where psi_i = Log(Exp(theta_m)^T Exp(theta_i)). Jacobian dc/dtheta_m = -sum w_i A_i R_m^T J_l(theta_m) (from my derivation: dc/dtheta_m · d = -sum w_i A_i R_m^T d, and d = J_l(theta_m) δ → dc/dtheta_m = -sum w_i A_i R_m^T J_l(theta_m)). Newton: theta_m -= Jc^{-1} c. This converges quadratically to the exact floating-point root: at the root, c == 0 exactly (or ~1e-17). Same machine-level story. No improvement.\n\nFine — implement stagnation-accept and test empirically. Actually, hmm, one more consideration: the FD test at line 381 was written for the OLD map and PASSED with atol 1e-10 — the old map was closed-form (no iteration). For the new map, if the 1e-10 atol proves too tight empirically, the honest fix is to relax the atol for the FD comparison to a level that still discriminates (e.g. 1e-8) — an internal consistency tolerance, not a scientific gate. I'll note it in the handoff if needed.\n\nLet me now update the mean function with stagnation-accept logic, then write all test edits, then run.", "summary": []}
