{"id": "reasoning-37463748-29d1-5076-b0ac-a028c938a420-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350491248, "updatedAt": 1792350491248, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Three failures. The unit tests (counterexample, limits) pass — so the mean function and its G identity are correct. The map-level tests fail. Let me look at the details:\n\n1. `test_interp_map_finite_rigid_spin_covariance` — x_b_spun vs Q x_b mismatch. Let me see the actual numbers.\n2. `test_interp_transfer_preserves_physical_spatial_wrench` — M mismatch: ACTUAL [42.5, -87.8, 485.3] vs DESIRED [77.2, -12.4, 485.1]. Significant — the transfer is NOT wrench preserving. Hmm.\n3. Jacobian FD mismatch — J wrong.\n\nA common cause: my G_i or the map's forward/jacobian has an error. But the unit test of the differential identity PASSED (sum G_i J_l^{-1} = J_l(theta_m)^{-1} verified numerically!). So G_i is right. The transfer formula per the review: G_i^T v. But my wrench test computes M_after via spatial_moment_from_rotation_covector(theta_i, m_i) = J_l(theta_i)^{-T} m_i. The wrench preservation identity: sum_i J_l(theta_i)^{-T} G_i^T v = J_l(theta_m)^{-T} v? From the differential identity: sum_i G_i J_l(theta_i)^{-1} = J_l(theta_m)^{-1} → transpose: sum_i J_l(theta_i)^{-T} G_i^T = J_l(theta_m)^{-T}. ✓ So sum of master spatial moments from the rotation covectors = J_l(theta_m)^{-T} v = slave spatial moment from v. And the lever arms: sum x_i × w_i f_b vs x_b × f_b: sum w_i x_i ≠ x_b in general! x_b = sum w x_i + R_m r_0. So sum (x_i × w_i f_b) = (sum w x_i) × f_b ≠ x_b × f_b. Difference: (x_b - sum w x_i) × f_b = R_m r_0 × f_b.\n\nHmm — so total moment preservation requires the master moment covectors to include the lever arm to the attachment point. The review's transfer: \"transfer w_i*f_b to master translations and G_i.T*(m_b+A.T*f_b) to master rotation covectors\". And the review's OLD-map defect analysis: \"Delta_M = D.T*(m_b + A.T*f_b)\" — \"translation arms lose (R_b*r0) cross f_b when the slave force is moved to the weighted attachment point. For the common mean chart, J_b^{-T} A.T f_b equals that missing lever moment.\"\n\nSo the A^T f_b term in v provides the missing lever moment: J_l(theta_m)^{-T} A^T f_b = (R_m r_0) × f_b. So M_after = sum [x_i × w_i f_b + J_l(theta_i)^{-T} G_i^T v] = (sum w x_i) × f_b + J_l(theta_m)^{-T} v = (sum w x_i) × f_b + J_l(theta_m)^{-T} m_b + (R_m r_0) × f_b = (sum w x_i + R_m r_0) × f_b + t_b = x_b × f_b + t_b. ✓✓ \n\nSo the transfer SHOULD preserve. But the test says otherwise with a big error. So either my test's expectation is wrong or the implementation has a bug. Note the mismatch is large (75 Nm absolute, 6x relative) — not noise. Let me check my test: M_before uses x_b = node_position(slave) + u_nl[slave_u]. But WAIT — is the slave node's reference position X_b = sum w X_i + r_0? In the fixture: attach_xyz = w1·X1 + w2·X2; b_xyz = attach_xyz + r_vec. ✓. And u_nl[slave_u] = sum w u_i + (R_m - I) r_0. So x_b = sum w x_i + r_0 + (R_m - I) r_0 = sum w x_i + R_m r_0. ✓.\n\nHmm wait — but the deformed master positions x_i = X_i + u[m_u] where u[m_u] are the master displacements in u_nl — masters are uncorrected. ✓.\n\nSo M_before = t_b + x_b × f_b where t_b = J_l(theta_b)^{-T} m_b, theta_b = u_nl[slave_theta] = theta_m. ✓.\n\nM_after per master: x_i × f_i + J_l(theta_i)^{-T} m_i where f_i = w_i f_b, m_i = G_i^T v. \n\nHmm — wait: is the additive covector at master i converted with J_l(theta_i)^{-T}? spatial_moment_from_rotation_covector(theta, m) — check its definition: presumably J_l(theta)^{-T} m. ✓ (used in the old test with theta_bar).\n\nSo mathematically it should balance... unless the implementation's G_i^T v is wrong, OR the mean_state in the transfer is evaluated at u_nl while my test uses... the transfer uses u_current = u_nl ✓.\n\nLet me debug numerically — quick local script (no harness? it's numerical but it's a debug of a failing test... the ledger counts attempts through the harness. A tiny local numpy debug run — I'll run it through the harness as a diagnostic to stay compliant. Actually — hmm, \"diagnostics<=120\" per run; each harness run charges. A local direct python run would evade accounting — not compliant. Run debug scripts through the harness too.\n\nActually wait — first let me think harder about the covariance test failure, which might share the same root cause.\n\nCovariance test: x_b_spun vs Q x_b mismatch. Let me get the numbers from the evidence. Let me view the failure detail.", "summary": []}
