{"id": "reasoning-46fbed40-e987-5bee-8954-7a3e016e2622-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350555780, "updatedAt": 1792350555780, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Key findings:\n- The mean itself IS equivariant (err 2.7e-16) and satisfies the Karcher condition (1.3e-16). ✓\n- J vs FD: columns 0-2 fine; column 3+ bad at rows 42-47 — those are slave dofs of an entry. So the Jacobian correction blocks are wrong for columns ≥ 3.\n\nRows 42-47: slave u (42-44) and slave theta (45-47) of entry 0 presumably. Columns 3+: what are these q columns? The reduced dofs... The bad columns start at 3. Interesting: columns 0,1,2 are fine. What's special? Columns of T corresponding to... the first master's dofs? Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, maybe the issue: my `jacobian` uses `entry.master_theta_projs` P_i = T rows at master theta dofs. But the FD perturbs q; the map apply uses u_lin = T q + u0 — the master thetas respond via P_i. That's consistent...\n\nHmm, wait — rows 42-47 with column 3: the FD says J[42,3] differs. Let me think about what could be structurally wrong: the C_u block = A @ M + S @ theta_proj. M = sum G_i P_i. FD of apply: du[slave_u]/dq = A M + S theta_proj... \n\nOH WAIT. I see it! In `apply_kinematics`, the correction d_u uses `_nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_m) @ r_0 + cross(d_theta, r_0)`. The derivative of `_nmlr(theta_m) @ r_0` wrt q: d/dtheta [_nmlr(theta) r_0] = d/dtheta [(R - I - skew(theta)) r_0] = A(theta) + d/dtheta[-skew(theta) r_0]. -skew(theta) r_0 = -theta × r_0 = +r_0 × theta = skew(r_0) theta. d/dtheta = skew(r_0). So d/dtheta [_nmlr(theta) r_0] = A(theta) + skew(r_0). So d/dq [_nmlr(theta_m) r_0] = (A + S) M.\n\nAnd d/dq [cross(d_theta, r_0)] = d/dq [skew(theta_m - theta_lin) r_0] = skew(r_0) d(d_theta)/dq = S (M - theta_proj).\n\nTotal: (A + S) M + S(M - theta_proj) = A M + 2 S M - S theta_proj.\n\nBut the correct derivative is A M + S theta_proj (derived earlier). MISMATCH! Let me recompute carefully.\n\nCorrection c(q) = _nmlr(theta_m) r_0 + cross(d_theta, r_0), where _nmlr(theta) = R(theta) - I - skew(theta), d_theta = theta_m - theta_lin.\n\nc = (R_m - I - skew(theta_m)) r_0 + skew(theta_m - theta_lin) r_0\n  = (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_m) r_0 + skew(theta_m) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0\n  = (R_m - I) r_0 - skew(theta_lin) r_0. ✓\n\ndc/dq = A M - d[skew(theta_lin) r_0]/dq.\nskew(theta_lin) r_0 = theta_lin × r_0. d/dtheta_lin = ... theta × r_0: derivative wrt theta is the map theta ↦ skew(theta) r_0 = -skew(r_0) theta (since theta × r_0 = -r_0 × theta). So d/dtheta_lin [skew(theta_lin) r_0] = -skew(r_0) = -S.\ndc/dq = A M - (-S) theta_proj = A M + S theta_proj. ✓ (as before)\n\nNow via my code's decomposition:\nd/dq [_nmlr(theta_m) r_0] = (A + S) M   [since d/dtheta [_nmlr(theta) r_0] = A(theta) + S; verify: _nmlr(theta) r_0 = (R-I) r_0 - theta × r_0; d/dtheta = A + (-S)... wait: d/dtheta[-theta × r_0] = -S? theta × r_0 = -S theta... no: S = skew(r_0). theta × r_0 = skew(theta) r_0. Also = -skew(r_0) theta = -S theta. So -theta × r_0 = S theta, d/dtheta = S. And d/dtheta[(R-I) r_0] = A. So total d/dtheta[_nmlr r_0] = A + S.] ✓\nd/dq [cross(d_theta, r_0)] = d/dq [skew(d_theta) r_0] = d/dq [-S d_theta]... skew(d_theta) r_0 = d_theta × r_0 = -S d_theta. d/dq = -S (M - theta_proj).\n\nTotal: (A + S) M - S(M - theta_proj) = A M + S M - S M + S theta_proj = A M + S theta_proj. ✓✓\n\nSo the decomposition is consistent. Then why does J mismatch FD?? The jacobian code: C_u = A @ M + S @ theta_proj. Hmm — that matches. Unless... M is wrong!\n\nM = sum_i G_i P_i. Is G_i = dtheta_m/dtheta_i correct? The unit test verified sum_i G_i J_l(theta_i)^{-1} = J_l(theta_m)^{-1} — an equivariance consequence. But maybe G_i individually are wrong while the sum identity holds? The identity only tests the sum. Let me check G_i directly by FD in the debug: perturb theta_1 and see dtheta_m.\n\nWait — actually, hold on. Let me reconsider the differential derivation. I derived:\n\nd = sum_i Ĝ_i e_i, with Ĝ_i = R_m B^{-1} w_i A_i R_m^T, where e_i are SPATIAL increments, d spatial increment.\n\nThen parameter increments: δ_m with d = J_l(theta_m) δ_m; e_i = J_l(theta_i) δ_i.\n\nJ_l(theta_m) δ_m = sum_i Ĝ_i J_l(theta_i) δ_i\nδ_m = J_l(theta_m)^{-1} sum_i Ĝ_i J_l(theta_i) δ_i\nG_i = J_l(theta_m)^{-1} Ĝ_i J_l(theta_i). ✓ (as implemented)\n\nHmm wait — but is d = J_l(theta_m) δ_m correct? Perturbing the parameter theta_m by δ: R_m' = Exp(theta_m + δ). Exp(theta_m + δ) = Exp(J_l(theta_m) δ) Exp(theta_m) = exp(d) R_m with d = J_l(theta_m) δ. ✓ spatial increment d. ✓.\n\nAnd for masters: R_i' = Exp(theta_i + δ_i) = exp(J_l(theta_i) δ_i) R_i = exp(e_i) R_i, e_i = J_l(theta_i) δ_i ✓.\n\nSo G_i as implemented should be right... but wait — the A_i in Ĝ: A_i = J_l(psi_i)^{-1} where psi_i = Log(R_m^T R_i). In the code: `A = [_rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(psi) for psi in psis]`. ✓.\n\nHmm — but hold on: in the derivation, dc/dtheta_m · d = sum w_i (-J_l(psi_i)^{-1} R_m^T) d. Let me double check the sign of the R_m^T d transformation. R_m → exp(d) R_m:\nR_m'^T R_i = (exp(d) R_m)^T R_i = R_m^T exp(d)^T R_i = R_m^T exp(-d) R_i.\nR_m^T exp(-d) R_i = R_m^T exp(-d) R_m R_m^T R_i = exp(-R_m^T d) exp(psi_i). ✓ (using R^T exp(x) R = exp(R^T x)).\nLog(exp(η) exp(psi)) with η = -R_m^T d: = psi + J_l(psi)^{-1} η. ✓.\nSo dpsi_i = -J_l(psi_i)^{-1} R_m^T d. ✓.\n\ndc = sum w_i dpsi_i = -sum w_i A_i R_m^T d + sum w_i A_i R_m^T e_i = 0\nsum w_i A_i R_m^T d = sum_i w_i A_i R_m^T e_i\nB R_m^T d = sum_i w_i A_i R_m^T e_i (B = sum w A)\nR_m^T d = B^{-1} sum_i w_i A_i R_m^T e_i\nd = R_m B^{-1} sum_i w_i A_i R_m^T e_i = sum_i [R_m B^{-1} w_i A_i R_m^T] e_i. ✓Ĝ_i.\n\nOK so where's the bug? Let me look at the actual jacobian code again... \n\nOH. I see it. In `jacobian()`:\n\n```python\nM = None\nfor G, P in zip(G_list, entry.master_theta_projs):\n    if P.nnz == 0:\n        continue\n    term = sparse.csr_matrix(G) @ P\n    M = term if M is None else (M + term)\n```\n\nThat's M = sum G_i P_i ✓.\n\n```python\nC_u = sparse.csr_matrix(A) @ M + sparse.csr_matrix(entry.r_skew) @ entry.theta_proj\nC_theta = M - entry.theta_proj\n```\n✓.\n\nHmm. Then maybe the issue is in apply_kinematics vs the FD: the FD perturbs q → u_lin = T q + u0 → master thetas change → theta_m changes. But WAIT — the helper sets u_lin[m_theta] = distinct values AFTER computing u_lin = T q + u0. So u_lin is NOT in the image of T anymore! The FD in my debug script computes apply(T(q±dq) + u0) — which does NOT have the distinct master rotations overwritten... wait, no: T(q±dq)+u0 gives master thetas = T's values, not the overwritten distinct ones. The overwritten values only exist in u_lin!\n\nIn the debug script and the new tests, I FD via full_u(q±dq) = apply(T(q±dq)+u0) — but the state with distinct master rotations was u_lin (overwritten), which is NOT T q + u0. So the FD is evaluating the map at states WITHOUT the distinct rotations, while J was evaluated at u_nl (with distinct rotations). MISMATCH — the FD is meaningless!\n\nHmm wait, but in the debug output, columns 0-2 matched to 1e-11. If the FD states had different master thetas, J(u_nl) vs FD at a different state would mismatch everywhere... unless for columns 0-2 the map is insensitive... Hmm, actually wait: T q + u0 for q random — the master thetas from T q are small random values (~0.05 scale). J at u_nl (distinct 0.35 rotations) vs FD at Tq+u0 states (small rotations): for columns 0-2 they agreed to 1e-11?? That suggests columns 0-2 don't touch the mean-dependent parts... and columns 3+ do. Actually more likely: columns 0,1,2 correspond to... hmm.\n\nHold on, actually let me recheck: in the debug script the FD uses `T @ (q + dq) + u0` — the master theta entries of THAT vector are T's small values, NOT the overwritten 0.35 etc. But apply_kinematics computes the mean from the master thetas of the input vector — small valu