{"id": "reasoning-96729709-3c4f-553a-9a12-07bc5862636d-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792350831427, "updatedAt": 1792350831427, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The 8th test failed, and the 11th test hung until timeout. No stderr. The pytest output with -q shows dots; the F is the 8th test. Test order (file order):\n1. test_mpc_descriptor_preserves_model_fingerprint[eccentric]\n2. test_mpc_descriptor_preserves_model_fingerprint[interpolated]\n3. test_rigid_body_motion_zero_strain_up_to_45_degrees[eccentric]\n4. test_rigid_body_motion_zero_strain_up_to_45_degrees[interpolated]\n5. test_diffeomorphism_and_constraint_residual_postcheck[eccentric]\n6. test_diffeomorphism_and_constraint_residual_postcheck[interpolated]\n7. test_lever_arm_jacobian_and_geometric_hessian_match_finite_differences\n8. test_reduced_coupled_tangent_matches_central_finite_differences[eccentric] → F\n9. test_reduced_coupled_tangent_matches_central_finite_differences[interpolated] → .\n10. test_cantilever_stiffened_shell_finite_rotation_solve_and_wrench_balance[force-eccentric]... wait parametrize order: (route, control_mode) — \"eccentric-force\"? The decorator order: @parametrize(\"route\") above @parametrize(\"control_mode\")... cases: eccentric-force, eccentric-displacement, interpolated-force, interpolated-displacement. Position 10 = eccentric-displacement? Hmm the 10th dot then 11th hangs: interpolated-force or interpolated-displacement.\n\nWait — 8th FAILED: test_reduced_coupled_tangent_matches_central_finite_differences[eccentric]?? That's the DIRECT route — legacy behavior! The direct route should be bit-identical to the old map... unless my geometric tangent changed direct behavior. Let me think: for direct entries, covectors(thetas) = [G^T v] = [A(theta_1)^T f_b + m_b] (G=I). D[0][0] = d/dtheta_1 (A^T f_b + m_b) = H_b (old) + 0. So K_geom should equal old theta_proj^T H_b theta_proj... BUT with a subtlety: the old code SYMMETRIZED H_b per entry and used central FD with h=1e-6 on rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian directly. My new code FDs the covector function which includes the mean computation — for direct, mean short-circuits exactly (single master) → covector = A^T f_b + m_b exactly. D = central FD → same H_b. Then term = P^T D P. Same as old. Hmm.\n\nBUT — one difference: the old code had `if entry.theta_proj.nnz == 0: continue` and skipped entries with tiny f_b; new skips when both f_b and m_b tiny. For direct with f_b tiny but m_b nonzero: old skipped (no geometric term); new includes d(G^T v)/dtheta = d(m_b)/dtheta = 0 → zero anyway. Fine.\n\nAnother difference: my FD of the covector uses h=1e-6 — same as old H_b FD. Same.\n\nHmm wait — actually there's a REAL difference for direct: the old H_b was FD'd via `rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(wp, r0).T @ f_b` — the derivative of A^T f_b wrt theta. My new: FD of [A^T f_b + m_b] — same derivative for the A part. Identical.\n\nSo why did [eccentric] fail?? Maybe the failure is a tolerance issue in the J-FD (rtol 1e-8, atol 1e-10) — for the DIRECT route the map is unchanged, so J is unchanged... unless... hmm wait, is it unchanged? For direct entries, my jacobian computes C_u = A @ M + S @ theta_proj with M = G P = P (single master, G=I). C_u = (A + S) P = delta_A P ✓ same as old. C_theta = M - P = 0 → skipped ✓. So J identical for direct.\n\nreduce_force for direct: f_mod[m_theta] += G^T v + w const = v + S^T f_b - m_b = A^T f_b + m_b - S f_b... wait S^T = -S: v + S^T f_b - m_b = A^T f_b + m_b - S f_b - m_b = A^T f_b - S f_b. Old: delta_A^T f_b = (A + S)^T f_b = A^T f_b + S^T f_b = A^T f_b - S f_b. ✓ identical.\n\nHmm wait — BUT the old code put it at f_mod[slave_theta] and mine at f_mod[m_theta]. For direct single master: slave_theta row of T = P (theta_b = theta_1). m_theta row of T = P too. Same contribution P^T (·). ✓ identical.\n\nSo direct is bit-identical... then why F? Unless the failure is in a LATER part of the test — the net_reduced_force FD comparison (K_red vs K_fd, rel_err < 1e-6) or the symmetry check, or the \"err_without_geom\" assertion. OR the test failed due to something environmental (e.g., the geometric tangent FD noise for direct... no).\n\nHmm — wait, actually maybe test ordering isn't file order... pytest orders by file definition order. Position 8 = the eccentric variant of test_reduced_coupled_tangent... Let me just rerun that single test with -x and full traceback to see. It's a regression run ≤600s but budget remaining is 187.9s — a single test should take ~10-20s.\n\nAnd the hang at test 11: which test is 11th? After test_reduced_coupled_tangent[interp] (10th... wait let me recount:\n\n1. fingerprint[eccentric]\n2. fingerprint[interpolated]\n3. rigid_body[eccentric]\n4. rigid_body[interpolated]\n5. diffeo[eccentric]\n6. diffeo[interpolated]\n7. lever_arm_jac_hessian\n8. reduced_tangent_fd[eccentric] → F\n9. reduced_tangent_fd[interpolated] → .\n10. finite_rotation[force-eccentric]? parametrize order: the decorators are @parametrize(\"route\", ...) then @parametrize(\"control_mode\", ...)? Looking at the file: \n\n```\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"route\", (\"eccentric\", \"interpolated\"))\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"control_mode\", (\"force\", \"displacement\"))\ndef test_cantilever_stiffened_shell_finite_rotation_solve_and_wrench_balance(\n```\nThe LAST decorator applied first... pytest orders: for stacked parametrize, the last one varies fastest? Actually the ordering: params are ordered with the last decorator first in the ID: \"eccentric-force\", \"eccentric-displacement\", \"interpolated-force\", \"interpolated-displacement\". So:\n10. finite_rotation[eccentric-force] → .\n11. finite_rotation[eccentric-displacement] → HANG?\n\nHmm — eccentric-displacement hung?? That's the DIRECT route with displacement control... The wrapper fix (recover_initial_load_factor) affects displacement control. Hmm — or the hang is in a later test. 240s for: tests 1-9 took ~30s (previous full module was ~40s for 22 tests)... test 10 ~10s... test 11 hung >180s.\n\nWait — actually, maybe the hang is the interp finite-rotation solve: with the new mean, the solve may converge differently (slower or diverging → max_iterations × num_steps...). The interp-displacement case: 8 steps × 25 iterations, each iteration with assembly ~... could take long if iterations don't converge (cutbacks). Or infinite loop?! The DC solver with the new map — hmm, the DC adaptive subdivision with max_control_cutbacks... A hang would be bad (unbounded loop). The DC1 fix bounded zero-span retries... but is there an unbounded loop elsewhere? The mean iteration is bounded (100). The DC solver: subdivision bounded by cutback count.\n\nActually — wait. The previous run of this module (before my changes) took ~40s total for 22 tests (both modules 52.9s). The 4 finite-rotation solves (force/displacement × direct/interp) were part of it. If the interp-displacement case now takes >180s, something about the new map makes the solve much slower (e.g., Newton not converging → cutbacks → many more assemblies). The interp route at 30° rotation with the new mean — the tangent is exact (FD-verified), so Newton should converge quadratically... but the geometric tangent is now FD-based with 12 mean evaluations per entry per iteration — 3 entries × 12 = 36 mean computations per geometric tangent call, each ~5 iterations of 3×3 ops — negligible vs assembly.\n\nHmm, but the fixture has nx=4, ny=1 → 10 shell nodes... small. Assembly per iteration maybe 0.1-0.5s. 8 steps × 25 iters × ... ~100s worst case. Plus the wrench-balance part reassembles. Could just be slow.\n\nLet me first see the actual failure of test 8 with a focused run, and time the finite-rotation tests individually. Budget: 187.9s left. Careful: each run charges. Plan:\n1. Run test_reduced_coupled_tangent[eccentric] alone with traceback (-x, timeout 120