path geometric tangent once more:\n\nOld code:\n```\ntheta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\nH_b = rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity, f_b)\nterms.append(entry.theta_proj.T @ sparse.csr_matrix(H_b) @ entry.theta_proj)\n```\nNew code: D[0][0] = FD of covector = d/dtheta_1 [A(theta_1)^T f_b + m_b] = H_b + 0. BUT — the FD in my code: covectors(plus/minus) with thetas = entry.master_rotations(u) — perturbing thetas[j][c]. For direct, thetas has ONE element. covector = G^T v with G=I → v = A^T f_b + m_b. FD → H_b exactly (same central difference, same h). Then term = projs[0].T @ D @ projs[0] = P^T H_b P. Old: theta_proj.T @ H_b @ theta_proj. For direct, theta_proj == P (same rows? theta_proj = T_csr[slave_theta], P = T_csr[m_theta]. For direct w=1: slave_theta row of T = 1.0 × m_theta row → identical). ✓ Same.\n\nDifference: old symmetrized H_b (0.5(H+H^T)); new symmetrizes the TOTAL (sum of terms). For a single entry single term: same.\n\nHmm — so direct geometric tangent identical. J identical. reduce_force identical. So reduced_tangent_fd[eccentric] should behave EXACTLY as before (it passed before). Something else must be wrong. Unless the F is not reduced_tangent_fd...\n\nWait — maybe pytest orders parametrized differently... Or maybe my count is off because test 7 (lever_arm) comes AFTER diffeo but there might be additional tests I forgot. Let me list test order precisely: grep def test_ gave: 194 fingerprint, 218 rigid_body, 309 diffeo, 348 lever_arm, 381 reduced_tangent, 480 finite_rotation, 618 split_restart. ✓ my count.\n\nHmm, but wait — did reduced_tangent_fd[interpolated] really pass? \"........F..\" — positions: 1-8 dots... no: 8 dots then F: positions 1-8 are dots (pass), F at position 9?? Let me recount the string: \"........F..\" = 8 dots, then F, then 2 dots. So F is the NINTH character → test 9 = reduced_tangent_fd[interpolated]! I miscounted. So:\n- Tests 1-8 pass (including reduced_tangent_fd[eccentric] ✓ legacy intact).\n- Test 9 = reduced_tangent_fd[interpolated] FAILS.\n- Test 10 = finite_rotation[?first case] passes.\n- Test 11 = finite_rotation[?second case] HANGS (>180s).\n\nThat makes much more sense! The interp reduced-tangent FD fails — the new map's K_red vs K_fd mismatch. And an interp finite-rotation solve hangs.\n\nWhy would reduced_tangent_fd[interpolated] fail? The test asserts:\n1. J vs J_fd: rtol 1e-8, atol 1e-10 — the mean iteration noise issue I worried about! FD noise ~1e-16/2e-7 = 5e-10 > atol 1e-10. Possible failure.\n2. Symmetry < 1e-12 — K_geom symmetrized ✓; J^T K J symmetric ✓.\n3. K_red vs K_fd rel_err < 1e-6 — K_geom is FD-based (h=1e-6) with mean noise; K_fd uses eps=1e-6 on the full net_reduced_force (which includes assembly + mean). The mean noise in net_reduced_force ~1e-16·scale... K_fd noise ~1e-16/2e-6 = 5e-14 — fine. K_geom's own FD error: central difference truncation O(h²)=1e-12 relative + mean noise 1e-16/2e-6=5e-14... fine. So rel_err should be small... unless the geometric tangent is WRONG (not the derivative of the reduce_force correction).\n\nHmm wait — there's a subtle inconsistency: my geometric tangent FDs the covector function covectors(state_thetas) = [G_i^T v] — this is d of the MASTER-SIDE covector contribution. But the true K_geom = d/dq [delta_T^T f]. delta_T^T f = sum_i P_i^T [G_i^T v + w_i(S^T f_b - m_b)]. The derivative: sum_{i,j} P_i^T [d(G_i^T v)/dtheta_j] P_j — the constant term w_i(S^T f_b - m_b) has zero derivative ✓. So K_geom = sum P_i^T D_ij P_j ✓ — matches my implementation. And the FD test in my new test (test_interp_map_jacobian...match_fd) PASSED — K_geom vs FD of corr(q) to 1e-6! So the geometric tangent IS consistent with reduce_force.\n\nSo what fails in reduced_tangent_fd[interpolated]? Possibly the J vs J_fd at atol 1e-10 (mean noise), or... the test also asserts `err_without_geom > 5e2 * ||K_red - K_fd||` — if K_red - K_fd is tiny, this is fine. Or the K_red vs K_fd rel_err < 1e-6: K_fd FDs net_reduced_force which 