\"<control_mode>-<route>\"? The ID is built from the parametrize decorators in REVERSE order (bottom-up): the bottom decorator's params come first in the ID? I recall IDs like \"eccentric-force\" when route is the top decorator... I genuinely don't remember.\n\nJust run the 4 cases individually with short timeouts. But budget! 183.8s left. Each case ~10s if converging. Run all 4 in one pytest with --timeout? Not installed probably. Alternative: run with harness timeout 100 and pytest -v so I see which case hangs; the harness kills at 100 and I read the -v output progress. Cost ≤100s. Hmm — that's half the remaining budget.\n\nAlternative plan: skip the finite-rotation coupling tests for now (they're my owned module but the EP acceptance is the priority), run EP half interp first (~40s), then come back if budget remains.\n\nActually — wait. Think: which case hung? If it's an interp case, the EP interp case might also hang → burning budget. If it's the eccentric-displacement case, the wrapper fix might be implicated (I touched the DC wrapper!). Hmm — the wrapper change: checkpoint_path now includes `or bool(recover_initial_load_factor)`. For the finite-rotation displacement cases: recover_initial_load_factor is False (no initial state supplied) → no change. The forwarding adds a kwarg the original solver accepts ✓. So the wrapper change is inert for these tests.\n\nThe finite-rotation displacement case: control = uz target -0.45 on the shell tip, 8 steps, 25 iters, tolerance 1e-9, corotational work_conjugate. Previously passed in ~5-10s. With the new map: direct route unchanged (bit-identical map) → eccentric-displacement should behave identically. So the hang is likely an INTERP case (interp-force or interp-displacement) — the new kinematics change the equilibrium path; the solve may now need many more iterations (each Newton iteration = full corotational assembly on the 4x1 shell strip + beam... maybe 0.5-2s per assembly with work_conjugate) → 8 steps × 25 iters × 1