{"id": "reasoning-b22c6e9e-400b-5e69-ab57-6d07a5a094f1-0", "sessionId": "341d9e81-6e08-2061-eb77-520faea41e81", "turnId": "turn-542b409187d41a418292618329863619", "createdAt": 1792351058227, "updatedAt": 1792351058227, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The dash printed as replacement char — the file may not be valid UTF-8, or the console encoding mangles it. The repr shows '\\ufffd' replacement char, meaning reading with encoding=\"utf-8\" hit invalid bytes? Actually io.open with errors='strict' would raise on invalid UTF-8. It didn't raise, so the file IS valid UTF-8 but the Windows console (cp1252?) mangled the print. repr should escape non-ASCII... repr of a str shows the character literally if printable. The em dash is printable, so repr shows it, but console encoding replaced it with '?'/'\\ufffd'. So the string likely does contain U+2014 and my earlier match should have worked...\n\nWait — earlier I opened WITHOUT encoding=\"utf-8\". On Windows, default locale encoding is cp1252. Reading UTF-8 em dash (E2 80 94) as cp1252 gives \"â€\" plus 0x94 → some chars, not U+2014. That's why the match failed! The file is UTF-8 but Python defaulted to cp1252.\n\nFix: open with encoding=\"utf-8\" both read and write. Redo the script with encoding=\"utf-8\".", "summary": []}
